> MS didn't force the Navy to put NT on their ship; the Navy did.
> Don't bash a product because it is inappropriately chosen


While I definitely agree with you, you have to consider that MS can be blamed
for trying very hard to give NT an image of a robust operating system worthy
of life critical applications.
I'd say borderline false advertising. 

I recently read some MS documents which pertained to "zero maintenance NT" and
I was amazed at the amount of brainwashing in them. I'd say worse than the
good old IBM in its heydays of brainwashing experience that was better than
the CIA's :-)

And MS certaintly has the attention of the medias and is doing everything it
can to attract those important mission critical applications because it needs
them to show off its NT system
and increase sales. It is a gamble MS is taking because the quicker they get
those critical apps in production, the more the odds of a PR nighmare for MS
when news breaks out of the failures of NT.

So yes, you can blame the sheep for blindly following the path. But you might
also wish to blame the sheppards who guide those sheeps.



