massimo@mercurio.iet.unipi.it (Massimo Macucci) writes:
> However, every week or so, a new problem has started appearing: the
> message "cant get mbufs" appears on the console, and, unless the
> machine is rebooted right away, a hardware reset becomes necessary,
> because everything get completely frozen.

The kernel memory pool is being exhausted; to fix it, you need to
increase the number of page table entries available to the kernel
memory allocator map.  Edit /sys/machine/mips/vmparam.h--towards the
end you'll find a section something like

#if (PHYSMEM/10 < LOW_WATER)
#define KMEMUMAP (LOW_WATER*256+KMEMSLOP)
#else
#if (PHYSMEM/10 > HIGH_WATER)
#define KMEMUMAP (HIGH_WATER*256+KMEMSLOP)
#else
#define KMEMUMAP (PHYSMEM/10*256+KMEMSLOP)
#endif
#endif

Change that to something like

#if (PHYSMEM/10 < LOW_WATER)
#define KMEMUMAP (2*LOW_WATER*256+KMEMSLOP)
#else
#if (PHYSMEM/10 > HIGH_WATER)
#define KMEMUMAP (HIGH_WATER*256+KMEMSLOP)
#else
#define KMEMUMAP (PHYSMEM/5*256+KMEMSLOP)
#endif
#endif

which doubles the PHYSMEM factor for machines with less than 100 MB
of RAM.  Build and install a new kernel and reboot.
-- 
Dan Riley                                         dsr@mail.lns.cornell.edu
Wilson Lab, Cornell University      <URL:http://www.lns.cornell.edu/~dsr/>
