Jay Maynard wrote:
> 
> Looks like I'm 0 for 2 on terminal servers. I now have a DECserver 100
> (DSRVA-AA) and a Xyplex 4550 with 4 TSRVM-J8 boards, both of which start and
> appear to work fine, and both of which appear to be downline loaded from a
> host. Argh.
> 
> Is there a way I can get software for either or both legally and without
> spending a fortune? This is, after all, for my personal use, and I've got
> $70 invested in the two of them. Hopefully, I won't have two doorstops when
> I'm done.

The DECserver-100 software is too old to be found on a Consolidated
Distribution, AFAIK. All your really need is load image (I don't recall
the name), then enable line service on your load host's Ethernet
circuit. Try for DECserver-200. You can find that on some (VERY!) old
Consolidated Distribution CDs.

Xyplex Servers loading from VMS require what's known as a "parameter
server". They do not store their parm's locally, rather, when you DEFINE
something (as opposed to SET), they upload the parameter definition(s)
to the load host via the Parameter Server. The "NVRAM files" are stored
on the load host. Lose them, and you're screwed.

Enjoy your door-stops / paper-weights / ...

