From: John Johnstone <johnstone@tridentusa.nspam.com>
Subject: Re: Digital 21" monitor flicker advice please
Date: 1998/07/31
Message-ID: <35C1D4B2.3B4B21B9@tridentusa.nspam.com>#1/1
X-Deja-AN: 376752525
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 7bit
Organization: Trident Computer Resources
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=us-ascii
Mime-Version: 1.0
Newsgroups: comp.sys.dec

Maybe this is in a monitors FAQ somewhere.  If not, it should be.  :-) You
probably have got magnetic interference in the locations where the monitors
are flickering.  We had an unbelievably difficult time trying to determine
the source of a similar problem here.  It was finally determined to be
magnetic interference coming from the electrical wiring in the walls.  I 
actually thought it was due to a cheapo monitor being used until we replaced
it with a Digital VRT17 and it still exhibited the problem.  Later on, we
also found this problem in a second place in the building.

All monitors are susceptible to magnetic interference.  Two factors make
monitors more susceptible:  resolution (scan rates) and size.  When you 
increase both, you'll be more likely to have a problem.

Due to many subtle factors, the electrical wiring can produce much more
intense fields in certain arrangements.  If you are really ambitious, try
carrying an affected monitor around the room with a long cable if you have
one and watch the results on the screen.  We actually did that here to solve
our problem.  We nearly had to take the monitor completely out of the office
for the interference to go away.  The office furniture had to be completely
rearranged to find a better place for the monitor.  Makes you wonder about
all the talk about health effects from low level EMF!

Hopefully you won't need it, but there are industrial monitors that are
available with magnetic shielding options that are designed just for
problems like this.  It may make someone quite unhappy to rearrange an
entire office to accomplish it, but you will have to try repositioning the
affected monitor until the problem improves.  Maybe just turning the
monitor 90 degrees, maybe moving it to a entirely different place in the
room.  It might take a lot of experimentation.

As a last resort, if you can't get any better results, you might want to
actually use a 60Hz refresh rate.  Even though it can be annoying to look
at, the flicker it produces has a different appearance and might be less
annoying than the flicker that you're seeing now.  It results from refresh
rates that are higher or lower than the 60Hz AC power wiring.

On newer monitors, there is a setup feature called Moire Cancel.  It doesn't
affect flicker but it can help with strange visual pattern problems that
also occur from magnetic interference.


