Ok, time to get specific.  By device, are you talking disk or other?
If it is disk, and you have spent the money for ASE and multiple machines
to run it on, I hope to hell you have a redundant disk farm (ie. HSZ70).
In the case of a disk, so what, big deal, and all that.  Just keep going.
If you have some other unspecified device that is screwing the server up,
then ASE will relocate the service to another server. 

By signal, I assume you are meaning some error in the logs will signal ASE
that there is a problem (pretty standard way to do it).  This isn't a
matter of configuring the kernel, but rather having a proper monitoring
system set up.  the monitoring system will inform ASE to relocate the
service.  Once the service is off, whether you shut you system do or not
is unimportnat, since your program is no longer running on that server.

does that answer your question?

