X-Deja-AN: 376293353
Organization: Geologisches Institut, Freiburg, Germany
Newsgroups: comp.sys.dec


We want to backup our project data from our UNIX workstations (operating systems: Digital 
UNIX 4.0B, IRIX 6.2) to CD-ROMS. We must be able to read the data from

-	UNIX
- WINDOWS NT

We have a CD-Write-Device on a Windows NT station, the UNIX machines are connected 
via NFS. 

When we wanted to create a CD with our UNIX data, we got the following problems:

1. Windows NT is not fully case sensitive. Under UNIX, you can have two files 

/directory/FILE 	and 
/directory/file 		(two filenames in the same directory differ only in upper/lower-case).

With our NFS software ("NFS Maestro Solo"), we can open these files from WinNT 
separately, but when we want to copy them to a WinNT disk, the content of the second file is 
copied over the first (or it is discarded; you can choose). This is because WinNT cannot have 
two filenames in the same directory differ only in upper/lower-case.

We have project data with thousands of files that only differ in upper/lower case. We cannot 
rename them because of Quality Assurance reasons.

2. The data and the directories must be readable from UNIX and WinNT.

We want to have the data as backup medium for our UNIX workstations, and our clients want 
to be able to read them from their WinNT PC. With the CD-writing software we used 
("Adaptec Easy CD Creator"), we got CDs that could not be read from IRIX 6.2 and only 
showed 8-character filenames from Digital UNIX 4.0B.

Is there a software under Windows NT that can do what we need? We would prefer this so 
that we needn't buy an extra CD-Write station for UNIX.

Or, when we have to write the CDs from UNIX, which software must we have so that the CDs 
can be read from WinNT?

thank you for any help & comment!

regrads Bernhard



