Ultimately, Microsoft is better able to determine if NT is able to handle
the rigors of a "mission critical" application.

If someone told you not to buy XYZ brand rope for rock climbing because it
wasn't strong enough to support your weight, but company XYZ marketed it as
a rock climbing rope, and sold it to you as a rock climbing rope, and you
used it, believing that company XYZ knew more about the strength of their
rope that the person telling you it wasn't strong enough.... If you fell
and were paralized, would you not blame the company that produced,
marketed, and sold you a rock climbing rope that wasn't capable of living
up to the claims the company made for it?

Sure, you could blame the person for not stopping you from buying it
because he knew better, or you could blame the person who decided to buy
the rope anyway (you, in this case), but you're definately going to want to
hold the company XYZ responsible for selling a defective product, false
advertising, etc.

