Well, I bought one when it first came out, nd used it for several years,
until I was a DEC employee and had access to better stuff thru employee
purchase. I eventually donated mine to a charatable .org and took the
writeoff. I don't know if they still have it or not.

IIRC, the computer was about $1200 with its 4KW memory. Serial and parallel
cards were about $100 each. Heath wanted $2500 for a DEC 64KB board, I got
third party for $995. I also got a second hand dual density floppy drive for
something like $1000 and a 3rd party controller for another $500 or so.

Expensive by todays prices, of course, but out of line then, I don't think
so. Rember we're talking 1977 prices here...

And it computed circles around the TRASH-80 which just started shipping
about the same time, and anything else on the home computer market back
then. The fact that it could do my model rocket flight simulations AT ALL
put it way ahead of anything available then. And a real time multi tasking
operating system that is better than any home computer 20 years later.

I still run RT-11 on my home PDP-11s, mostly on a PRO.

	Bob Kaplow	



