In article <6pdbrj$5gv@nenevr.demon.co.uk>, simon@nenevr.demon.co.uk.NOJUNK (Simon Coombs) writes:
|>
|>Q1. I have gained a couple of VAXstation 3100's but have no idea what 
|>    model they are - The front panel doesn't hint at anything! They both 
|>    have KA42-A CPU's, one is model VS42A-BD and the other is VS42A-BW.
|>

VAXstation 3100 Model 30 or Model 40. The Model 30 is the smaller (less tall)
box that has one internal tray for drives etc. The Model 40 has two such
trays. The electronics are identical (although the M40 probably has a beefier
power supply).

|>    Both machines have RX23 drives, one has 1 internal SCSI connector,
|>    the other has 2.

The SCSI interface is provided by an add-on board. This might be a SCSI/SCSI
board that gives you SCSI-A and SCSI-B, or it might be a SCSI/ST506 board
that gives you one SCSI bus and an attachment for a standard floppy.

|>Q2. According to test 50, these units (which are currently diskless!) have
|>    only a SCSI-A adapter, no SCSI-B - and yet there is still an external 
|>    high-density 68 pin connector, and one machine had a terminating plug
|>    sat in it which gets warm when powered up - thus it's terminating 
|>    something?? My question - does this mean that SCSI-A is being brought
|>    out onto that connector?

Yes - except I think it is SCSI-B.

|>Q3. Any recommendations as to the model of CD-ROM I should attempt to get
|>    for this thing? (Assuming that the answer to Q2. is YES) Presumably, 
|>    RRD-40 drives are a little hard to come by, nowadays!!
|>

I expect that RRD40s are not that hard to find - but you would be better off
with an RRD42 or later. The RRD40 required a special caddy that nothing else
used and which is a bit hard to find.

Antonio

