In article <35baf6b9.0@rainbow.hrz.uni-siegen.de> engel@numerik.math.uni-siegen.de writes:
>   : Multia SRM console   BL5 V3.8-3, built on Aug 10, 1995 at 03:22:55
>   : *** Warning NVR/TOY battery is low.  System may not boot ***
>   : Testing memory from 8 to 64 meg...
>   : Loading ARC firmware...
>
>   : And that's all folks.  After leaving it on for 24 hours, it never came back
>   : and still does the same thing on startup.
>
>   I have a Multia here with exactly the same symptoms. Definitely the 
>   battery that powers the CMOS clock/RAM has lost power and the Multia gets
>   confused about that. Try starting it with battery disconnected, this should
>   at least get you into SRM console. You might also see such funny effect
>   like detection of a 125 MHz CPU (instead of 166 here), a message about
>   timer interrupts and more ... I didn't replace the battery in my Multia
>   yet, so I can't definitely say if this fixes the problem.

I just went through this exercise.  When I disconnected the battery and
let the NVR drain fully, it brought up the ARC console, which complained
loudly about the NVR settings being inconsistent.  But, it did start.

I replaced the battery - since I could not get the appropriate
Ray-O-Vac #840 4.5 volt alkaline battery, I stuck three AAA
batteries together and soldered on the old battery's connector;
works just fine.  After that, I booted up back into ARC, reset
the NVR to factory defaults, fixed the time, system config, and
environment variables, and told it to switch back to SRM.  The
system works fine now.

--Pat.



