In article <6pdbrj$5gv@nenevr.demon.co.uk>, simon@nenevr.demon.co.uk.NOJUNK (Simon Coombs) writes:

:Apologies in advance for these questions - comp.sys.dec seems to get a lot
:of enquiries like this one, and yet none of them seem to quite answer any
:of the questions *I've* got!...

  Please see the OpenVMS Frequently Asked Questions (FAQ) for pointers to
  a website with MicroVAX and VAXstation information...

:Q1. I have gained a couple of VAXstation 3100's but have no idea what 
:    model they are - The front panel doesn't hint at anything! They both 
:    have KA42-A CPU's, one is model VS42A-BD and the other is VS42A-BW.
:
:    Both machines have RX23 drives, one has 1 internal SCSI connector,
:    the other has 2.
:
:    According to the OpenBSD/vax netboot test program, it's boardtype
:    a000004, which is VS3100 model 10 - 48. Anyone got any ideas?

  Based on the KA42-A identifier, these appear to be either VAXstation
  3100 model 30 or model 40 systems.

  The model 30 and 40 differ only on the size of the enclosure -- the model
  40 (and the subsequent model 48 CPU upgrade) uses a taller enclosure, with
  5 internal storage bays and three rows of vent slots on the side.  The
  model 30 (and the subsequent faster model 38) uses a short enclosure, with
  3 internal storage bays and two rows of vent slots on the side. 

  The model 30 and 40 CPU is the same, as are the model 38 and 48 CPUs.
  The model 76 is the fastest CPU in the series.

  Many early VAXstation 3100 series systems have only "VAXstation 3100" on
  the front -- systems after the model 30 and 40 -- the models 38, 48, and
  76 -- will generally have the model number on the front medallion.

:Q2. According to test 50, these units (which are currently diskless!) have
:    only a SCSI-A adapter, no SCSI-B - and yet there is still an external 
:    high-density 68 pin connector, and one machine had a terminating plug
:    sat in it which gets warm when powered up - thus it's terminating 
:    something?? My question - does this mean that SCSI-A is being brought
:    out onto that connector?

  It would certainly appear that way, yes. :-) 

:Q3. Any recommendations as to the model of CD-ROM I should attempt to get
:    for this thing? (Assuming that the answer to Q2. is YES) Presumably, 
:    RRD-40 drives are a little hard to come by, nowadays!!

  RRD-series CD-ROM drives support the required 512 byte block size, and
  the necessary integration and testing work has already been performed
  for these drives.  I would look to an RRD42 or later series drive...

  A system disk larger than 1.073 gigabytes should *not* be used on any
  member of the VAXstation 3100 series system.  Data disks can be larger,
  depending on the restrictions and requirements of the operating system.

