Jerome Fine wishes to also add a note or two:

Back in the early 1980s, DEC already had  BOTH  the hardware and
software already in operation.  In fact by the late 1970s, I was told,
by one of the large distributors for DEC products, that DEC had been
approached to market a PC type of system based on the VT52.
While I have never seen that type of implementation, I have seen
a VT103 which dates back to the early 1980s and probably came
out in the early versions as a 4 * 2 Qbus backplane mounted
within the almost empty compartment on the right-hand side.
Later versions had a 4 * 4 Qbus backplane.  While DEC
refused to make a 22 bit backplane available, I have seen,
in operation, a 22-bit backplane with a dual 11/73, 4 MBytes
of memory, a DLV11-J (4 serial ports) and an MFM 3rd
party disk controller (with BOOT ROMS).  The hard disk
drive of 70 MBytes (5 1/4" full height) was under the tube
(it was a very tight squeeze).  And the power requirement
were probably on the border line since only 16 Amps
were available on the +5 volts.

But there were plans to add a DHV11 with 8 serial lines and
to replace the MFM controller with an ESDI controller at
which point it was intended to place a fan beside the hard
disk drive and run both the hard disk drive and the fan
from a PC power supply from outside the VT103.

While I agree that in 1980, only the PDP-11/23 was available,
the 11/23 was certainly far faster and better than a PC-XT.
In addition, a number of operating systems such as RT-11,
RSX-11 and RSTS/E (to mention only the major ones) were
also available.

And finally, I have heard that one enterprising fellow actually
modified the backplane so as to accommodate a uVAX II
within a VT103.

So, DEC had  BOTH  the hardware and the software as
far back as 1980.  Only marketing decisions were missing.

On the other hand, would IBM have licensed Microsoft
if there had been a vision of the future?  Doubtful in my
personal opinion.

Thus I suspect that  BOTH  IBM (#1 at the time) and DEC
(#2 at the time - or was that a bit later?) missed the boat on
the size of the PC market for likely the same reason - I believe
it was mostly just arrogance as well as having chosen a sales
model we now know as low volume, high margin.  Thus,
there really was nowhere for the PC to fit into the sales
plans of either IBM or DEC since the PC requires, as
we have seen, high volume low margin marketing (or at least
that is how the PC became successful).  Perhaps both
IBM and DEC rejected the PC as a viable system back in 1980
since neither company could understand how a simple system
such as the XT could sell in vast numbers and who needed a
computer on the desktop in any case (as was said about
the telephone as well)?  As well, the anticipated power of
the PC was likely not seen back in 1980 even though the
exponential growth in power of all computers had been
apparent for the previous 15 years.

IBM seems to have survived at this point - the next 10 years
will tell the tale.  DEC is gone and I also will miss the company
that developed so much good CPU hardware, even though
it was overpriced.  AND,  the software concepts always
seemed to be among the best, although, like Microsoft,
the implementation left much to be desired;   BUT  very
much unlike Microsoft, DEC did keep the same basic
structure for the operating system and over a period
of sometimes 2 decades, did in most cases manage to
produce excellent and stable operating systems.

Sincerely yours,

Jerome Fine




