I have a hard time believing that a Divide by Zero error crashed NT.  Even
DOS handles this one (by killing the offending program).  More likely the
Divide by Zero is being blamed when something else is what actually caused
the crash.  Misplaced blame in military systems is common, especially since
said systems are usually politically driven (and careers are made and broken
on them).

That said, military weapons systems are inherently real-time, and NT isn't.
NT is a poor choice for a weapons system for that one reason alone and to
make it work as a real-time system would require significant kernel driver
coding.  NT is notoriously vulnerable to kernel level driver crashes, which
is why display drivers (which install as part of the kernel) are so
frequently implicated in NT problems.

There are many places where NT is appropriate - real-time weapons systems
aren't among them.

Also, I don't trust the source of this report (Government Computer News)
since they have consistently bashed anything Microsoft, regardless of
whether MS disserved it.

These comments are my own personal beliefs - derived from experience, and
don't reflect the official position of the US Department of Defense or the
US Air Force.

Mike Ober.
Former Air Force Officer.







