>->(particularly a VAX/VMS box) , and adding the same amount of RAM to an NT
>->box.  PC RAM is so cheap these days that you simply can't cost-justify
>->paying an expert to sit down for nnn days looking at your system, just to
>->tell you that you need more RAM to deal with memory bottlenecks.  Buy the
>->RAM first, and then call in the NT expert if that doesn't solve your
>->problem.   I can get 128 MB PC RAM for $240.  Try beating that price
getting
>->RAM for a VAX/VMS box.


You're comparing apples & oranges here.  RAM for a VAX (depending on the
model) may be ECC, which means that up to a two-bit error can be *corrected*
without nasty side effects.  With PC memory, you're lucky if you even have
parity.  Parity *may* tell you if you have a single bit error, but you're
dead in the water anyway.  Can you say "Blue screen of death?"

I've seen many an occasion where a VAX reports a memory error, and continues
running happily until a *scheduled* shutdown for repair.  Try that with your
PC.

Two adages apply here: You get what you pay for, and cheaper isn't always
better.

