Some performance numbers for the 21264 were recently posted on the DEC web
site albeit with no information on hardware configuration.  The figures
released are:

  AlphaServer 8400 6/575        30.3 SPECint95  47.7 SPECfp95
  ALphaServer 8200 6/575        30.1 SPECint95  44.8 SPECfp95

The clock rate would seem to be implied to be 575 MHz although on some Alpha
Server models the "slash number" is not exactly the clock rate.  This seems
somewhat disappointing since the original projections for the 21264 were >30
SPECIntbase95 and >50 SPECfpbase95 at 500 MHz (Look up the original micro-
processor report article on the 21264 at www.chipanalyst.com)

This is suprising since up to now DEC has been very good at hitting, or more
typically, exceeding performance projections.  Are there still bugs in the
complex out of order execution logic that force certain issue sequences or
performance enhancement features to be disabled for correct code execution?
Is the supporting logic or L2 cache in these first machines suboptimal?

My comments aren't meant to be derisive to the Alpha guys since even these
numbers put the 21264 far ahead of anything else.  It is not uncommon for
shipping CPUs to fall short of early projections (604, come on down!) but
I guess the DEC team inspired high expectations by batting 1000 on the 21064
and 21164.

Hopefully the official 21264 submission to the SPEC org for these machines
will be posted tomorrow and we can look up more info on hardware configuration
(L2 cache size, true clock rate etc).

All opinions strictly my own.

--
