"Mark E. Levy" <levy@family-safe.net> writes:

> You're comparing apples & oranges here.  RAM for a VAX (depending on the
> model) may be ECC, which means that up to a two-bit error can be *corrected*
> without nasty side effects.

Small correction here: The typical ECC codes - 32+7 and the equivalent 64+8 -
can correct any one bit error, can detect any two bit error, will detect a
large number of the three and higher bit errors, and are often set up to be
able to correct an aligned 4bit field (from one broken DRAM chip) stuck-at-one
or stuck-at-zero. Being able to _correct_ two bits errors is very costly -
AFAIR, the best code possible is 12+11, which can correct three-bit errors.

	Jan



