J. Buck Caldwell wrote:
> 
> Off topic a little bit, NetBSD supports Dial-On-Demand PPP. I've got a MicroVAX 3600 being used as a
> demand router for my network at home. When IP traffic is requested, it dials, and disconnects after
> 5 minutes of inactivity. Your pppD should be able to do this - NetBSD is mostly BSD.
> 
> Of course, it might help if we knew what type of *nix and hardware you were using...

If you had read and answered his post instead of `advertising' you 
would have noticed. He said:

>> Have just started to get into Unix 4.0D on a 500/400 and can't find a
>> way automate the ppp stuff to get dial on demand modem connection to my
>> ISP, (ie, click on Netscape get mail etc). The manual laughingly talks
>> about using tip to dialup and make the connection, but this is a bit of
>> a pain when i`m used to using DP4.0 on my aging Sparc machine to do it
>> all completely transparently.

>> Is there a way to automate this so the ISP gets dialled up without the
>> hassle of using tip ?. Have had a brief look at the DP4.0 sources, but 
>> a port to DecUnix looks seriously non trivial...

To at least partially answer the actual question - The man page for
ppp_manual_setup is  a "manual setup" not necessarily manual operation.
The 'chat' script in the line: 
    pppd /dev/tty01 38400 connect 'chat -v -f /etc/ppp/chat-script'
should be able to handle the invocation of the dialer. I do not know
if it will trigger off of IP activity automatically. PPP over ISDN
from the AXP 3000/300X (DU4.0) did work that way but I have only used
analog PPP to setup manually operated private links.

Charlie

