J. A. Sigler wrote in message <35B2ACA7.235C66E0@flash.net>...

>> It took me several days of poring through the manuals to find out how to
>> properly tune my VMS boxes.  The only tuning I had to do on my NT box was
to
>> add more RAM.  VMS: 0 NT:1
>
>Add more ram? I would not consider that tuning.  Only a necessary
>expense with NT.


Incorrect.  Price and compare the cost of adding RAM to a VMS box
(particularly a VAX/VMS box) , and adding the same amount of RAM to an NT
box.  PC RAM is so cheap these days that you simply can't cost-justify
paying an expert to sit down for nnn days looking at your system, just to
tell you that you need more RAM to deal with memory bottlenecks.  Buy the
RAM first, and then call in the NT expert if that doesn't solve your
problem.   I can get 128 MB PC RAM for $240.  Try beating that price getting
RAM for a VAX/VMS box.

VMS: 0
WNT: 1


>> >With OpenVMS, you could run File Services for PC's, Print Services and
>> >database applications and mail, all off of one engine.
>>
>> With NT, you can run file services, print services and database
applications
>> off of one engine as well, but why would you want to, when you can get at
>> least two x86 NT boxes for the price of one VMS box.  Hell, the cost of
>> putting TCP/IP on that VMS box is probably enough to buy another NT box!
>> VMS:0 NT:2
>
>Using cheap hardware is not recommended.  You must adhere to
>the NT HAL or you are asking for trouble.

I could configure an NT server, using HAL-recommended components, with file
services et al, for less than the cost of putting TCP/IP on one of my
VAX/VMS boxes.  If you don't think this is possible, I think you should
review the HAL.

VMS: 0
WNT: 2


>Indeed why would you wan to run file services, print services and database
apps off
>one engine? I mean after all, you don't want to down all of your critical
services/apps
>during the obligatory BSOD's which will occur.


"Running all apps of the same engine" is usually a necessity with a VMS box,
because of the hardware/software capital and maintenance costs involved.  It
is not a necessity with NT.  And my NT server hasn't BSOD'd in months
(though we did have to restart this morning after a month of operation for
another reason).  So there :-).

VMS: 0
WNT: 3


>And lets mention the topic of security just for fun.  I need not go into
detail
>here.  :)
>
>VMS:4 NT:0      Yes, VMS gets an extra point for security.


I will agree with you partly here, because VMS offers more control over
access and account management than NT does (try getting a list of all users
who haven't changed their password yet from NT for example).  However, NT
incorporates DOMAIN-level security, while that on VMS is limited to one
machine.  No more requirement to manage nnn accounts for one person over nnn
VMS machines. This is a big plus in my books for NT.

VMS: 0.5
WNT: 3.5


Brian Steele







