Les Cargill (lcargill@worldnet.att.net) wrote:
: Hi, all. 

: I'm researching for a friend whether there are 
: replacement disk controller/subsystems for an
: ancient  PDP-11 running RSX. Is there? :)

Well, we really need more information, in particular whether the hardware
is Unibus or Q-bus.  "Ancient" could be a problem.

I think you could still find used controllers at some of the used
equipment vendors.  For example, I just bought a couple of RA92 disk
drives in April from Computer Clearinghouse in Rochester, New York.  It so
happened that I didn't need a controller, but I'm sure I could have got
one.  (There are lots of other used equipment vendors, and any list I make
for you will be incomplete.  Other places I have spoken to include E.L.I.,
Midwest Systems, Newman Computer Exchange, and Twin Cities Digital.)

On the other hand, older controllers could be harder to find.  SCSI
controllers are available, but still quite expensive, ie there is little
difference between the new price and the used price.

What kind of disk drives are currently being used?  If you upgrade to
SCSI, you almost certainly will have to use DUDRV.  If your current disks
do not use DUDRV, you will need to be using a recent enough version of RSX 
that DUDRV exists, and you will need enough of the RSX distribution to be
able to create one.

If your disk types and RSX version are very old, I found somebody a few
years ago that made solid state replacements for RKxx and RLxx drives, but
that is probably not cheap either.  After all, who would want to replace
small (slow) disk drives with small (fast) disk drives?

- Rob

