    I think I saw 64MB parity DIMMS going for about $100 each at onsale.com
last weekend, so that would come to $200 to add 128MB to my Alphastation 500
(actually it has a 256 bit wide memory bus so I'd have to buy 256MB for
$400 using this model, but you get the point). 


> Is that for ECC RAM? If so are there sufficient tools to tell you there are
> or are not memory problems?

   Most low-end Alpha systems ( Alphaserver 1200 and below ), use
industry standard parity RAM modules ( SIMM or DIMM ). I believe the
same is true of the newer microVAX systems, but I've never actually 
bought one of those. Any good quality generic module should work 
in these systems (YMMV). Parity memory generally costs 15-20% more than
non-parity because it needs banks of 9 chips instead of 8 ( and
the volumes are lower because most low-end system builders don't care
if their system can detect/correct memory errors ). Some Alpha systems
go a step farther by implementing an ECC scheme using the parity bits
in a memory bank, so errors are not only detected but many can be
automatically corrected.

   So yes, memory for a "PC class" VMS box would cost a bit more than
memory for a bottom of the line PC clone - but you're not comparing
apples to apples there. If you were to buy the parity modules for your
PC they would cost the same as the Alpha memory. Of course many
brand-name Wintel servers also require parity memory, so for those
systems the cost of memory upgrades is a wash. I'll grant you
most of the "Honest Freds house of Clones" systems don't require
parity memory, in fact I've run into some that choke if they
see it. We can leave the debate over the foolishness of using non-parity
memory in a mission critical server to another day :-)

