If I remember correctly, yes.

 
> then it goes through the diags countdown (maybe this is not PERFECTLY exact, but
> something like this):
> 
> F...E...D...C...B...A...9...8...7...6...5...4_.3_.2_.1?
> 
>  ? 7    80A0 0000.4001
>  ? 6    80A1 0000.4001
> ?? 1    00C0 0011.700E

"7" and "6" are your SCSI buses.  Are they terminated?  The lines you
show are for the SCSI controller itself.  The next line in each case
would give information about the devices on the bus.  It would be
usefull to see these lines.

FYI: You probably have a dual SCSI controller.  The "A" bus is the
internal bus.  This requires a special cable which connects to a 100pin
connector on the SCSI controller.  Without this cable, the bus is not
terminated properly.  The "B" bus is the external bus.  You mention that
you have the terminator for that, so the bus should test OK.

"1" is the Ethernet.  Probably, your Ethernet connection is not
terminated.  Connect it to a network or put a terminator on it and the
problem should go away.


