In article <35B7CBA3.FBA7700@intop.net>, "J. S. Havard" <enigma@intop.net> writes:
:What are they?  All I know is boot.

  On the MicroVAX II KA630 console, the BOOT command is pretty much all
  you really need to know about, and it's the only command that's used
  with any regularity on most KA630 systems.

  There are examine (E) and deposit (D) commands, as well as some other
  primitive commands -- the KA630 console command set is very primitive.
  
:                                   And I don't know what the switches
:do.  How do I figure out what the ether addy of my DEQNA is, t 50
:doesn't work on it, of course, that was for a vs2000.

  Replace the DEQNA with a DELQA or (S-Box) DESQA controller, or remove
  it from the configuration.  No recent OpenVMS versions -- including the
  hobbyist OpenVMS VAX V6.1 release -- will support that controller.

  To examine the Ethernet address of a Q-bus Ethernet controller on a
  MicroVAX II, look at the low bytes of the output from the following
  console command:

    >>> e/p/w/n:5 20001920

:  I can't boot the
:thing because of a defective drive, or controller, but seeing as how I
:can sometimes do a b/3 DUA0 and get to the sysboot> prompt, who knows.

  The B/R5:1 boots to SYSBOOT, while B/R5:3 stops at SYSBOOT and loads
  XDELTA.

  Seeing that you can sometimes get to the SYSBOOT prompt, it would appear
  that there is a Q-bus configuration problem -- one or more modules have
  incorrect CSR and/or interrupt vector problems, or that the copy of OpenVMS
  on the disk is hosed, or there is a flakey disk or system problem.  Having
  a bad CSR can hose any attempt to bootstrap, and it can also hose the
  system when a Q-bus autoconfiguration is performed.

