Gregg E Economou wrote:
> You'd think that after the first failure the Navy would have socked
> microsoft with a HUGE lawsuit. (heck, at least the Pentagon still has a
> bigger budget than gates!)

Applications dictate reliability much more than the operating system. The
problem with 
Windows is that a lot of applications were written by teenager-mutant-nitendo-programmers
and the latter don't have a solid rock foundation on which to build, and add
this to their
inexperience, the relaiability suffers, but companies can buy stuff dirt cheap.

Running the app on UNIX would not have meant randowm fireworks with the ship
sending out all its missiles at unpredictable times. That is an application
matter, not an operating system one.

However, when you do write an app for an operating system which does not
provide good solid system services, then your application is bound to suffer
unless you have used a robust system before and build your own robust system
services which are lacking on the new system.

This is where the teenage-mutant-programmers lack because they have not worked
on solid systems such as VMS and don't know what sort of system services they
are missing on their new system.



