Correct.

From what I remember about this incident from the comp.risks digest, the
problem was related to the entry of a zero into a database package.  The
ship was towed to port where - and this is the bit that scared me - the problem
took 2.5 days to fix.  Naturally the key system had no adequate backup.

Much as we're all shocked to learn the navy uses NT to steer it's boats, this
means that in this case it's a pure application issue, unless you count the 
lack of a decent BACKUP utility as an OS issue :)

> However, when you do write an app for an operating system which does not
> provide good solid system services, then your application is bound to suffer
> unless you have used a robust system before and build your own robust system
> services which are lacking on the new system.
> 
> This is where the teenage-mutant-programmers lack because they have not worked
> on solid systems such as VMS and don't know what sort of system services they
> are missing on their new system.

I'm not sure I entirely agree with this.  After all, the above bug is unlikely
to have been avoided on VMS by resorting to better system services.  If the
database application was written in, say, Oracle then it'd have broken just 
as well on either OS.

Most bugs in programs are down to the programmers, much as we'd like
to blame faults in the underliying operating system.

What may be true is that the modern clicky-pointy, fill-in-the-forms methods
of programming lead to less thorough debugging, and that with these methods
people do not develope such strong debugging and testing skills.  

That this nicely distinguishes VMS programmers from NT programmers is, in
part, a coincidence, or at least a consequence of the lack of such development
tools under VMS.

Something else that may have an effect is the use of a GUI.  With a GUI there 
is scope for increased numbers of paths through the code than there are with 
command line programs.  Data is more easily entered out of sequence and
in inconsistent forms.  This too can cause GUI programs to have more bugs
than there command line equivalents (this last from someone who ported a VMS
application to Windows :)

I speak as a solid VMS developer who is quite convincingly putting together
some teenage-mutant-programmers-alike code under NT :(

