Mime-Version: 1.0
Newsgroups: comp.sys.dec,comp.unix.osf.ofs1

I'm trying to install an alphaPC 164SX.  It has an IntraServer
wide SCSI controller that digital unix won't recognize until
you install driver patches via setld.  You can not boot from
a disk on this controller without the drivers installed.
I installed these patches on a different machine, a dec 3000 model
400 running Digital UNIX 4.0A and built a kernel from the GENERIC
config file.  I used the GENERIC config file so I could include
_everything_ since I have no idea what drivers are needed on this
alphaPC.

The drivers for the SCSI card declare some symbols that occur
in the cam_siop.mod module and cause multiple symbol declarations
and a failed kernel build, so I had to hack a few things out of
the GENERIC config file in order to avoid these errors and
subsequent _undefined_ symbol errors that occurred when I removed
the cam_siop module.  Here's the lines I commented out:

> #cpu          "DEC4000"
> # bus         lbus0   at ibus0
> # bus         siop0 at lbus0 slot 0   vector siopcointr
> # bus         siop1 at lbus0 slot 1   vector siopcointr
> # bus         siop2 at lbus0 slot 2   vector siopcointr
> # bus         siop3 at lbus0 slot 3   vector siopcointr
> # bus         siop4 at lbus0 slot 4   vector siopcointr
> # controller          te0     at * vector teintr
> # controller          te1     at * vector teintr

Anyway, the kernel finally built successfully and I moved it to
/vmunix, removed the disk and put it on the alphaPC to boot from it.

The kernels starts to boot and configure devices then gives the
following errors:

   No kernel console driver installed
	.
	.
	.
   panic (cpu 0): System does not have a console configured.

Anyone know what the problem is and how to fix it?
I was originally booting with a graphics console (monitor) but
couldn't see what the error message was since it cleared the screen
and printed out the kernel crash dump messages.  So I unhooked the
monitor, keyboard and mouse, changed the value of console to
"serial" via the SRM console and hooked up/configured a serial
terminal.  Then I has able to see the above message.  I assume this
is the same message it was giving on the graphics display but
can't be sure because I couldn't read it there.

Any help/info would be greatly appreciated.

Thanks,

-Jerel



