	Tom,

	As you are aware ... Redmond invented the Internet.  Likewise,
	Sun may have invented clustering.  Just kidding.  But as others
	have pointed out, what passes for clustering in other worlds
	just isn't what we would call clustering.

	The crux of the debate is this:

		If your version of clustering supports a user on 
		Node A writing into an arbitrary file while many users
		on Nodes B, C, D, E, F and G are reading from that 
		same file AND you are RELYING on an APPLICATION
		to maintain file sanity, is it clustering?

	Trade rags and the other camp says yes.  I say *partly* because it
	isn't a cluster common filesystem, it is an RDBMS acting as 
	a traffic cop.  I would like to place any home grown I/O intensive 
	application in that cluster and watch it work.  NFS serve it across
	the cluster?  Please.  Hence, Sun's focus on single big machine and
	yes indeed it scales quite well.

	VMS engineering/marketing is quick to point out with Galaxy,
	applications will run unmodified.  Of course you may wish to
	re-write your apps to take advantage of the new ICC API but
	the point is backwards compatibility is paramount.
	
	Mr. Andrew "Clusters Don't Scale" Harrison is in for a surprise
	by year-end.  VMS Clusters more than scale today and will be rocketing
	RSN.

