Miro Jurcevic wrote:
 
> The Alpha Multia has an unique version of the CPU which contains
> additional chip functions and not others. If you are suggestng that the
> LINUX OS is intended for use as a file server or other data service then
> you may find that the Multia falls short.

the first part of the statement is correct - the Universal Destop Box
(Multia) does use a modfied version of the EV4 cpu - it uses an LCA4
(166mhz) or LCA45 (166, 233) cpu.  The LCA reduced system bandwidth
(64bits rather than 128bits) but integrated PCI and memory management on
chip.  The standard integer perfomance of a 166mhz LCA is about that of
a 66mhz Pentium, the 233 coming in around a pentium 100.  For floating
point performance they are equivalent to ~100mhz and 180mhz pentium
respectively.  The reduced bandwidth effectively starves the processor.

They work fine as small linux fileservers, gateways, or X terminals. 
Their built-in SCSI and tulip chip based ethernet makes them useful
little boxes.
  
> 
> It is however possible to install the AXP chip from the Alphastation 200
> series and run at a higher speed - this however is only for the hot-dog
> developers that don't mind sparks coming out of the exhaust.

no - this is not possible - the Alphastation 200 was an EV4/EV45 based
machine - LCA and EV4 are not pin compatible.

