Andrew Harrison SUNUK Consultancy wrote:
> Arne Vajh�j wrote:
> > I just downloaded the results.xls from tpc.org and found:
> >
> > Digital Alphaserver 8400  102541 tpmC
> > IBM RS6000 SP309           57053 tpmC
> > HP 9000 V2250              52117 tpmC
> > SUN E6000                  51871 tpmC

> 3 of these numbers are using clustered
> systems. TPC-C unlike most applications
> scales well across nodes in a cluster.

Which 3 and how do you define clustered ?

> Compare actual applications benchmarks like
> SAP which does not scale well on a cluster
> and you get a totally different story.
> 
>                         SAP SDs         CPUs employed
> Sun E10K/250            6030            27
> HP V2200                5320            16
> Digital 8400 5/625      2001            10
> 
> The CPUs in the E10K were slower than the
> CPUs in either the V2200 or the 8400.

> > When will the E10000 be TPC-C tested ?
> 
> I actually don't know but in a way its
> irrelevant, the E10K leads the market on
> SAP and Baan which are applications benchmarks
> and both of which are OLTP applications which
> TPC-C is supposed to model.

I do in no way like TPC-C, because it is my impression that
a test is to expensive and to cumbersome, so that testing is
only done occasionally.

BUT, the only benchmarks that are worth anything are
benchmarks that are available to the public in source,
so that it can be installed on any system and all results
can be verified by whoever wants it. Tests should be
designed by someone independent without any particular
financial interest in the results.

If not, the MHz is as good a benchmark as anything.

It is obvious that *if* the E10000 did scale well for 64 CPU's,
then it would set a new TPC-C record.

Arne



