Note: comp.os.vms added to follow-up list...

In article <35b51eb5.55750201@news2.abraxis.com>, 
    		rdervan@BLAH.abraxis.com (Richard Dervan) writes:
> We're getting a "PEA0 port transition failure" and crash upon booting VMS when
> it's trying to join the cluster.  This *just* started happening.  DEC says
> it's a corruption issue with our system drive.  I think it sounds like
> hardware.  Does anyone have any ideas?  The current process during the crash
> is SACONFIG.EXE (Stand Alone Config).

        Let me see.  If  DEC  says  it's  file corruption (i.e., "system
    drive"),  why  do you insist it's hardware?  I'm sure you have  good
    reasons, I'd just like to know what they are...

        FWIW, PEA0 is a _software_ device (a port driver) which does not
    talk directly to the hardware.  Rather (oh, I hope I've got this one
    right!) it's one  or  more  _class_  drivers  that  talk to whatever
    cluster interconnects are available (e.g., Ethernet, FDDI, CI, DSSI,
    SCSI,  Memory  Channel) with each actual "network" adapter having  a
    particular class driver (the CIPCA on our Alphas uses  SYS$PNDRIVER,
    for  example,  and  creates  a  PNA0  device).  [I could easily have
    "port" and  "class"  reversed  above...corrections  hereby  actively
    solicited.] Note that PEA0 increments its error count for every time
    a node leaves the cluster, whether via a normal shutdown or a crash.
    Also  transient network interruptions that cause "lost connection to
    node xxxx" followed by "reestablished connection to node xxxx"  will
    also increment the error count on PEA0.

        The point is, unless SHOW ERROR  indicates errors are a hardware
    device,  e.g.,  PNA0,  or  similarly, that  ANAL/ERROR  shows  error
    entries for a particular cluster adapter, I'd guess the  problem  is
    in the software, i.e., disk corruption.

        -Ken
