Steve Roylance <roylance@helix.mgh.harvard.edu> writes:
> OK, here's a red herring - maybe off topic, maybe not...
> Does anyone know where I might find the specs on DEC's
> Local Area Transport protocol?  I'm interested in LAT
> terminal servers and would like to able to analyse LAT and
> maybe implement LAT for non-digital platforms (linux)

  Why? LAT is pretty much a dead issue, except on older terminal servers that
don't implement TCP/IP - most non-DEC servers now charge extra for LAT or
don't include it at all.

  LAT is a DEC proprietary protocol and DEC wants to charge per-port license
fees to people who use it. I think the licensing program is handled by Mer-
idian Software.

  You can get some info on an early, obsolete level of the LAT protocol from
the patent applications. DEC [unintentionally] sold copies of the V5 LAT
spec for a while - I think Alan Frisbie might still have some copies avail-
able.

  The source listings for some DEC operating systems (such as RSTS/E) include
the host side of LAT; the VMS listings are censored and don't have all of it.

