I have used NT 3.51, 4.0, Solaris x86 and FreeBSD on PC
hardware for a wide variety of services.  I can say that as
a server OS on intel PC hardware, FreeBSD is the best
solution availbable.  It is reliable, and it has the most robust
TCP/IP implementation of any OS I have worked with.  Under
heavy load it responds much better than Linux, SCO, or
Solaris x86.  NT does not even come close.  I have run NT and
FreeBSD on identical hardware, running the same services, and
the performance advantage of FreeBSD is amazing.  I have seen
some of my FreeBSD servers remain in operation for over 200 days without
having to be touched under fairly heavy load.  Can anyone
imagine a heavily loaded NT server with a 200 day uptime?

I think NT is good for the desktop, especially in comparison to
other offerings from Microsoft.  As far as the server room is concerned, I
want to see UNIX, VMS, etc.


