Larry,

	You must understand that when Andrew attempts to denigrate VMS
	he always comes at it from a Unix point of view.  Sun knows
	one thing and one thing well and that is Unix. and so everything
	is viewed through Unix colored glasses.  He still seems to think that 
	Lock Traffic is THE major impediment to scaling.  I would bore you
	with some of his earlier quotes but why bother.  Thankfully, the
	VMS engineering folks came up with the following in conjunction 
	with Oracle to show just how well VMS scales and how out of
	touch dear Andy is. . .

	Keep in mind that where we read commits in an OLTP context we
	can safely say "commits of the C variety".  As we know, short
	of having a fully audited config , TPC forbades talking in terms
	of C and D type transactions.  As you peruse the whitepaper, it
	is clear that 10 I/O per commit is akin to the "C" variety.

http://www.openvms.digital.com/openvms/whitepapers/oltp/vlm_p.html#vlm_14

"During these OLTP experiments, write caching on the HSJ controller dedicated to
LGWR (Oracle's Log Writer process), showed substantial benefits under the
heaviest loads and highest transaction rates. Higher transaction rates (commit
rates) translate into higher demands for I/O throughput for LGWR. Prior to
write caching, LGWR reached a maximum of 350 commits per second. Removing the
bottleneck by upgrading the HSJ controller to use write caching boosted the
throughput rate for these joint OTLP tests by over 25% to over 440 commits per
second. Write caching also provided plenty of spare capacity for LGWR
throughput. With capacity to spare, the project engineers implemented a series
of further optimizations that increased aggregate throughput by another 25%.
Peak Oracle commit rates reached 600 commits per second with substantial LGWR
logging capacity still remaining thanks to write caching optimization."


	600 commits per second, i.e. 36000 "transactions of the C variety
	per minute".  Not bad for an older config of 440 MHz CPUs.  Note
	that it is estimated 625 MHz CPUs would do 700 per second, 42000
	per minute.  Using old HSJ40s, show me some HSZ70s next time ;-).
	2500 writes per second?  Nice.

	Here is where Andrew really lets his Unix understanding of clustering
	cripple him.  Unix clustering is not clustering as VMS folks know
	it.  Unix clustering is quite different.  Larry, in the thread you
	replied to, Andrew also writes:


"TPC-C is easy to partition both within the DBMS 
and at the client level. Many apps are not and 
if you get as in your example multiple clients 
attached to multiple nodes all hitting the same 
records then your scalability drops because as 
you quite rightly say clusters will resolve the 
contention slower than a single system. In this 
case instead of your 2 node cluster delivering 
2x the single node performance it may only deliver 
1.1 X."

	Sounds good on the surface, and all the Unix folks nod in agreement.  
	"Yup yup yup."  But it is far from true.  True in a Unix context, but
	ignoring a much more scalable OS:

http://www.openvms.digital.com/openvms/whitepapers/oltp/vlm_p.html#vlm_20

"OPEC's current conservative estimate for OpenVMS cluster scalability for OLTP
workloads similar to those used for these tests is that a two-node cluster will
scale at about 90%, and a four-node cluster will scale at about 80%. Further
extrapolations based on this indicate that an OpenVMS cluster using four
AlphaServer 8400 nodes could deliver over 1,900 OLTP transactions per second
for Oracle 7 using 440 MHz CPUs. This amounts to more than 6.8 million
transactions per hour."


	1.1 for a speed up Andrew?  Is that typical of a Sun FullMoon
	cluster?  Digital/Compaq VMS Engineering estimates 1.8 for a two node
	cluster.  Is that a bit higher than you expected?  At 1900 per
	second or 114,000 per minute, how is that?  Not bad , eh?  In fact,
	it recently took Digital 8 nodes to do a number less than that
	using Unix.  Scaling that you would expect from UNIX.  Gee, hope
	the bean counters don't stumble upon that.  Makes it hard to spend
	much more on Unix I suppose when the bean counters start poking
	around.

	Anyhow, does that help Andrew?  Do you understand now that VMS does
	indeed scale much better than Unix?  By the way, you haven't even
	seen scaling yet.  Galaxy ... APMP ... that will redefine scaling.


	Extra credit:

		In 200 words or less, explain why VMS scales better
		than Unix.

	Bonus:

		Explain what the major differences are between Unix writes
		in a cluster environment versus VMS writes in a cluster
		environment and how that is reflected in cluster scaling.

				Rob


P.S.  Maybe if you partitioned your database differently it would scale better.




