The exchange below should be a reminder of some things:

While the worldwide web and usenet tend to be used predominantly in the
United States of America, and English is the primary language, both are
WORLDWIDE in scope.

1) If you're quoting prices other than US dollars, please make it clear in
the original post.  Don't assume that everyone is going to check and see
what  group you posted to, or what your return address is.  In this case,
the message was posted to four groups, only one of which had nz (New
Zealand) in the name.

2) If you see a post with less than perfect English, don't jump on the
poster about misuse.  I saw one case where someone made fun of a poster's
English, comparing him to Yoda from Star Wars.  The poster was from China or
Japan (I don't remember which), and I doubt that the one making rude remarks
could speak ANY Oriental tongue AT ALL.

Many "foreign" languages use a sentence structure that gives more prominence
to the verbs (what was done) than the nouns (who did it).  To them, it is a
mark of respect to make themselves secondary, and ENGLISH is "backwards".

3) If you're posting an item for sale/purchase in the USA or Canada, give
some sort of indication where you are.  You don't need to include your
street address, but naming your state, or noting "upper Calif" or "Toronto
area" can make a lot of difference.  Saving $20 on a 21" monitor doesn't
mean much once you factor-in 3000 miles of shipping charges, and some folks
might like to buy an item in person.  A 200 mile drive for a computer system
offered at a great price mught be a wise investment.

Toward a better WWWeb....

