> scales well across nodes in a cluster. 

A curious point of view, indeed.

Certainly if someone has a database engine built to ignore clusters
(anything ported in from Unix, for instance) scaling would be poor.

But take an application built to use RMS (or Rdb, I suppose).  What
is the cause of inefficiency there?  If there is high contention for
certain records, database pages or binary tree nodes, that contention
will be resolved more slowly than on a single system.  But such a
situation is slower on a single system than an application which has
been tuned properly.

So, saying that non-clustered machines are better at making the best
of a poor design, yes I would agree with that.

Larry Kilgallen



