Larry Kilgallen wrote:
> 
> In article <6on5k4$lci@flonk.uk.sun.com>, Andrew Harrison SUNUK Consultancy <andrew.harrison@#uk.#sun.#com> writes:
> 
> > 3 of these numbers are using clustered
> > systems. TPC-C unlike most applications
>                  ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^
> > scales well across nodes in a cluster.
> 
> A curious point of view, indeed.
> 

Not at all.
> Certainly if someone has a database engine built to ignore clusters
> (anything ported in from Unix, for instance) scaling would be poor.
> 

Most UNIX databases are now designed to utilize 
clusters. Oracle, Informix and Sybase all have 
cluster support. 

It generally isn't the DBMS but the application 
which dictates of a cluster solution will be 
scalable.

> But take an application built to use RMS (or Rdb, I suppose).  What
> is the cause of inefficiency there?  If there is high contention for
> certain records, database pages or binary tree nodes, that contention
> will be resolved more slowly than on a single system.  But such a
> situation is slower on a single system than an application which has
> been tuned properly.
>

TPC-C is easy to partition both within the DBMS 
and at the client level. Many apps are not and 
if you get as in your example multiple clients 
attached to multiple nodes all hitting the same 
records then your scalability drops because as 
you quite rightly say clusters will resolve the 
contention slower than a single system. In this 
case instead of your 2 node cluster delivering 
2x the single node performance it may only deliver 
1.1 X. 

In the DW space doing something like a complex 
join across multiple nodes in a cluster can 
also exhibit poor performance. 

> So, saying that non-clustered machines are better at making the best
> of a poor design, yes I would agree with that.
> 

The other more important point is that many 
applications are not supported at all on clusters.

Baan for example to not support any form of 
parallel database clusters. The applications 
actually work fine, Sun has run Baan benchmarks 
on a cluster but Baan do not support the config 
so few customers would take the risk.
 
Andrew Harrison
Enterprise IT Architect



