Newsgroups: comp.os.vms,comp.sys.dec

In article <6ooj3s$m11$1@news2.ispnews.com>, 
    			"Bob Young" <rcyoung@groupz.net> writes:
> I have a small cluster where one of the systems has been 'replaced', but
> where we have moved all the original system/user disks. The other member of
> the cluster is now unable to see the 'replacement'. The only change I can
> think of is the node now has a different hardware ethernet address.

        I think the key here is that you're  trying to add a new node to
    the  cluster  using the old system's _name_.  I say that because  of
    your use of the word "replace" and mention of moving all the  disks,
    presumably intact with no intervening Autogen (required to get a new
    SCS node name, etc.).

        Fortunately or not, a  VMScluster  has  a  good deal of "cluster
    memory" and will NOT allow a cluster member it "knew" with a certain
    hardware address to (re)join with a new hardware address (here, take
    "hardware  address" as a generic term...I'm not certain what item or
    items is/are used to make that determination, but I'd guess that any
    and all cluster interconnects on a given network, i.e.,  CI  station
    addresses on the CI, Ethernet hardware addresses on a given network,
    etc.).  I'd actually be surprised if when the new system boots, it
    actually "joins" the cluster.  What do the console messages say?

> If this seems right, how can I 'repoint' the older system to the
> 'replacement', or is there more to it?

        The solution is to  do  a  full  cluster  shutdown and reboot (a
    rolling  reboot  won't work since the "memory" of the  old  hardware
    persists).

        -Ken
