"Michael D. Ober" <mdo@wakeassoc.com.nospam> writes:
|Don't confuse ease of use with ease of development.  Windows (or any other
|GUI environment) is a nightmare to develop under when compared to developing
|for a non-windowing terminal.  However, a well written GUI application is,
|in theory, much easier to use.  I say "in theory" because there are a lot of
|worthless programmers running around.

Ease of use is not necessarily the same as ease of learning.  A well
written GUI application may be easy to learn, but tedious to use over
a long period of time (notice that GUI interfaces like MacOS and
Microsoft Windows have shortcuts to try to reduce the hassle).

GUI interfaces are easier to show other people, but command lines
are easier to describe (e.g. in email).

