Both GUI's and command-lines has its advantages.

GUI is easier to get started with. GUI is easier to remember when
you only use it infrequently. GUI is easier to use, if you are not
a computer professional.

Command-lines are more power-full, faster for the user using it
every day, easier to document, easier to use inlarge scale via
scripting and natural for a computer professional.

That is very generic. You can ofcourse write a difficult to use GUI
and a totally power-less command line interface. But if they are wriiten
as they should the above should be close to the truth.

To me that makes it obvious that GUI's are absolutely necesarry in
word-processors and spread-sheets for the 3/4 of users using them
sometimes. A GUIC may also be a good idea for OS management, if there
are no computer professional in the house.

It is also obvious to me that a command-line interface is optimal for
OS management.

It is my experience, that the optimal technology for word-processors
and spread-sheets for the user using them 8 hours each day, is the
combination of grahpical display with keystroke sequences.

Arne



