case the advantages of adding additional CPUs are 
considerable.

> In any case, I find it difficult to believe that mail processing
> is bound by processor availability.  I would expect that disk and
> network are more serious bottlenecks.
> 

It depends on how your mailserver is architected.
If the mailserver is multi-threaded then it should 
be able to use multiple CPUs and I/O may then 
be your bottleneck.

If its single threaded then CPU will probably 
be the issue since the other CPUs in the 
system will be idle and what you need is 
the fastest CPU you can get.

Of course NT runs on systems whose I/O 
capabilites are not good enough to blow 
out a candle so even if your mail server 
is well architected and multi-threaded 
you are probably out of luck anyway.

Regards
Andrew Harrison
Enterprise IT Architect



