Arne Vajh�j wrote:
> 
> Jan Vorbrueggen wrote:
> > jordan@Starbase.NeoSoft.COM (Jordan Henderson) writes:
> > > Well, nothing scales very well beyond 8 processors.
> >
> > Tell that to my 40 processor box with a speedup of 30 over the uniprocessor
> > case.
> 
> Scaling is higly depending on what kind of computing is to be done:
>   - many tasks / single tasks
>   - are programs build for parallel execution
>   - are the problems being solved suited for parallel execution
> 
> Some problems does not scale at all, so anythimg above 1 processor
> is just a waste. Certain mathematical problems scale very well, so if
> communication between processors are good and the compiler can generate
> the necesarry code, then it may scale well above 100 or even 1000
> processors.
> 
> If we can use Digital's and other companies product lines as an
> indication, then I would say that 4-6 processor was top in the
> old multi-tasking days, while 10-20 processors is top in todays
> multi-thraded applications.
> 
> Arne

This is still to much of a generalisation.

Firstly some single image, multi-threaded applications 
scale very well to many more than 20 CPUs, some petro 
chemical simulation examples I have seen exhibit 98% 
scalability to much larger numbers of CPUs than 20.

Secondly, some applications like DBMS improve throughput
by running multiple DBMS processes one on each CPU farming 
out the load across CPUS. In this case indevidual 
transactions may not run any faster, but the system is 
able to process multiple transactions at a time.

Thirdly, even in the second case DBMS's such as 
Oracle, Informix and Sybase have parallel query 
optimizers that can run a single query across 
multiple CPUs. We have some data mining applications 
running on Suns that use this sort of facility and 
get ~58x speedup on a 64 CPU machine, pretty good
scalability.

Finally, systems running general workloads 
like say file/mail/security/web services can 
also scale well to very large numbers of CPUs.

Regards
Andrew Harrison
Enterprise IT Architect



