This was part of IBM's design.  They wanted to design with chips that
were available in quantity.  Their choice to go with the 8088 was
strongly influenced be the availability of Intel's 8-bit support
chips.

But even then, software could have hidden the problems.  It's very
common for software to be used to mask hardware problems, but the lack
of a decent OS on the PC, and the tendency of PC applications to talk
directly to hardware, hindered that approach.

On the other hand, none of this would have made a difference, if it
were any other company.  Lots of other microcomputers had stupid
designs.  However, as soon as a micro had IBM's name on it, that's
what caught on.

> Not that PC IRQ priority is done in
