I remember my outrage at this obviously shortsighted design when Byte
magazine published the first PC hardware specs. At that time the Intel
support chips were  inferior to the Zilog chips for almost all functions,
and the interrupt controller was (still is?) one of the worst. Only one
output pin for 8 possible interrupt sources?! Get serious. The comparable
Zilog chip actually produced an indirectly vectored jump right to the
proper (one of 256) interrupt routine, all without cpu intervention.

Then there was the DMA channel restriction that was mostly a bus design problem.

In short, the PC design was far less than state-of-the art for its time,
and its predecessors still suffer from many of those basic defects. Good
software design may be able to lessen the impact of some of these defects,
but never erase them. And then there's the issue of actually getting that
good software....
