In article <6od6j4$6hg$1@news-2.csn.net>,
Michael D. Ober <mdo@wakeassoc.com.nospam> wrote:
>I wouldn't blame this one on NT.  Rather, blame it on poor IT contingency
>planning.  Any system can crash, yes, even VMS, with the correct sequence of
>external events.  It is the contingency planning that makes the difference
>of quick or painful recovery from the outage.

I believe that America Online recently had a similar problem where a power
fluctuation caused their NT servers to crash along with the Registry
information.

The Windows NT Registry is essentially, for those of you who are
unfamiliar with it, a hierarchical database of environment variables.
The Registry lets installed applications know where their
files are installed, what options are active for the applications, 
where mission critical data is installed, and other permanent or
temporary information.  If you lose your Registry, or if it becomes
corrupted, you may have to back up what data you can off the hard
drive, which may be hard to impossible because the program that allows
you to back up the data, restore the data, or access the data may no
longer function, reformat your hard drives, and reinstall the operating
system and all of your programs and data.  The Registry resides in a
system file that is locked while the operating system is running, and
you cannot back it up automatically.  As far as I am aware, the only
way to back up the Registry is to manually export the Registry to an
ASCII text file and then back up the ASCII text file.  If there is a
way to automatically back up the Registry, then I would like to know,
because that would be useful information to have.

I previously worked at a Fortune 500 company which I will not name
which lost access to over 20 Gigabytes of an Oracle database for a
period of several days, jeopardizing several months of work on a $250,000
data warehousing project, because the Registry was erased by the
network administrator during a system upgrade and there was no backup
of the Registry even though the Windows NT system was backed up
automatically every day.  The backup software could not back up the
Registry because, as I mentioned, the Registry was locked as long as
the operating system was running.  Another programmer kludged together
a Registry file from his own computer to regain access to the server,
but the system was increasingly unstable from that point onward.  (Word
to the wise: Back up empty directories, because one of those empty
directories may be the TEMP directory, and all sorts of mysterious
problems can occur as the result of a missing TEMP directory after
a hard disk has been restored with a backup of only non-empty
directories.  The operating system should inform you that the TEMP
directory is missing on start-up, but it does not.)

Backing up the Registry should be easier that it is.  The difficulty
in automatically backing up the Registry is an insidious design flaw
in Windows NT that has, can, and will wreak havoc on disaster recovery
plans.

Also, if banks kept track of money the way that Windows (any version)
keeps track of memory, then Bill Gates would be a poor man.  This
problem is most noticeable on servers that run continuously 24 hours
a day.  If you want to reclaim the memory that is gradually lost to
memory leaks, reboot your Windows NT servers periodically, perhaps
once each day.

-Ed L



