Another major factor is how the synchronization is implemented.
In general, on a uniprocessor, coarse grained synchronization
(like the VAX IPL) works well.  A finer grained locking strategy
(like the Trestle window system, where virtually every screen
object has its own lock) scales much better in a multiprocessor.
VMSers will be familiar with MPSYNCH overhead.

NT, I believe, suffers from coarse grained synchronization in =

the O.S., even though mechanisms are available to do much better
if the architects so desired.  =


> >
> > If we can use Digital's and other companies product lines as an
> > indication, then I would say that 4-6 processor was top in the
> > old multi-tasking days, while 10-20 processors is top in todays
> > multi-thraded applications.

In the days of the VAX 6000 systems, the XMI bus was usually =

saturated by 4-6 processors.  These days, the general purpose
servers with large CPU counts use switched interconnects
with much higher bandwidth.  I seem to recall readin that =

the forthcoming Digital^H^H^H^HCompaq Wildfire server will =

have a crossbar switch as its PMI.

