for systems with more than 14 processors has been high enough
to develop such systems.

They may be wrong. But it is my impression that IBM, HP, and SUN
also stays in the 10-20 range for let us call it conventional
systems (systems for general usage, not highly-specialized
number-crunchers for various simulations).

> Thirdly, even in the second case DBMS's such as
> Oracle, Informix and Sybase have parallel query
> optimizers that can run a single query across
> multiple CPUs. We have some data mining applications
> running on Suns that use this sort of facility and
> get ~58x speedup on a 64 CPU machine, pretty good
> scalability.

I note that SUN supply 64 CPU systems.

And then I am really surprised. Database applications are usually
very IO bound. So the capability of actually using 64 CPU's
puzzels me some. Are you doing computational intensive
manipulation on relative small data or do you have a gigantic
memory cache ?

> Finally, systems running general workloads
> like say file/mail/security/web services can
> also scale well to very large numbers of CPUs.

They do scale to some extent. And they are getting better.
But the external limitations in disk and net speed +
slowdowns due to the need for simultanous access to things
will always put an end to scalability at some point.

Arne



