Andrew Harrison SUNUK Consultancy wrote:
> 

> The fact that Digital does not ship more than 12 CPU
> machines
> (14 is hardly practical given the I/O, CPU, Memory tradeoffs
> required in the 8400) 

Correct!

> is much more to do with where Digital
> chose to spend their R&D dollars.
> 
> From external evidence (products) they havn't spent much
> on backplanes/interconnects or OS the bulk seems to have
> gone on the Alpha and the Alpha FABs none of which benefits
> SMP scaling.

The information DEC has **publicly** disclosed to date on Wildfire would
tend to indicate otherwise. Incidentally, all members of the next
generation of Alpha boxes implement crossbar switch technology. They
will appear in the Fall 1998 (low end and midrange) to Winter 1999
(Wildfire) timeframe.

In the interim, DEC seems to have done quite nicely with TurboLaser: for
a three year old platform (ancient in this industry) it is still selling
quite nicely.

> 
> As far as the market is concerned there is a large market
> for UNIX servers that cost over 1 million which Digital
> don't play in at all. IDCs market share numbers for
> this segment show 0% for Digital, Suns share of this
> market is worth ~1 billion dollars, simular to Digitals
> total UNIX systems revenues.
> 

Having worked for the above box-counter years ago, I have to question
the numbers. Any AlphaServer 8400 configured with 6 CPUs and a decent
amount of memory and storage will sell for more than a million, and such
configurations have shipped. Still, DEC remains a low-observable in the
very high end of the Unix market. The firm's total Unix systems revenues
are closer to $2B than $1B, though.
> >
> 
> Of course there are always limits the E10000 has limits
> as does the 8400, the E10000s are just much higher.

No doubt about it, the Starfire is a decent piece of hardware. The
dumbest thing (and this comes from a long list!) SGI ever did was
sell the Cray interconnect technology to Sun. Scott McNealy is laughing
all the way to the bank. As well he should!

Terry Shannon
