Edward Lee wrote in message <6ohj9j$89t$1@chinet.chinet.com>...

>If you want to reclaim the memory that is gradually lost to
>memory leaks, reboot your Windows NT servers periodically, perhaps
>once each day.


This reminds me of the advice I got from a VAX "expert" a few years ago -
"reboot my VAX once every two weeks to clear up any memory problems".  Still
wonder if that's really true or not.

If you're concerned about memory leaks, simply set up a non-privileged
account, and use it to log on to the NT server and view the Task
Manager.Performance screen, or perhaps check memory usage via the
Performance Monitor.  You'll definitely see changes in memory allocation if
you've got a process that's leaking memory on the server.

On average, we reboot our systems about once a month, whether it's necessary
or not.  A controlled shutdown/reboot is usually much better than an
uncontrolled one!

Regards,
Brian Steele







