Edward Lee wrote:

> <cut>

> The Registry resides in a
> system file that is locked while the operating system is running, and
> you cannot back it up automatically.  As far as I am aware, the only
> way to back up the Registry is to manually export the Registry to an
> ASCII text file and then back up the ASCII text file.  If there is a
> way to automatically back up the Registry, then I would like to know,
> because that would be useful information to have.
>

On my home system I use RDISK /S to create an ERD (Emergency Repair Disk) at
regular intervals, and especially after making configuration changes or
installing new software.I keep several generations of ERD.

I use ntbackup and it _does_ have an option to backup the registry, as have some
3rd party backup solutions (e.g. UltraBac, which I keep promising to get myself).

As for backup and recovery, I invested in a decent DAT drive from the word go. I
then created a 'bare bones' installation of NT in another directory.

If my main NT installation gets wrecked, I have 2 chances of recovery:

1. I can boot into my second installation of NT and access files which would
otherwise be locked.

2. I can simply wipe out the main \NT directory tree and then restore it complete
from tape. The last time I did this I was up and running in about 1 hour flat.

Incidentally, I do a full backup every week and then a differential one (all
files changed since last full backup) each day.
