> 
> Well, there was an 11/750 that was running scheduling and monitoring
> software for an extremely expensive peice of high-energy physics
> equipment.  The box stayed up so long that, so the story goes, DEC
> released a software patch to avoid reusing process-id 0 when the
> process numbers wrapped.

:-)

It is true that VMS systems can stay up forever. I will usually
recommend
a reboot every 2 or 3 month, not so much to avoid memory leak problems
(I have never seen a problem in VMS with that - and UCX and PathWorks
can be restarted witout reboot), but to test if SYSTARTUP_VMS.COM
is uptodate. If the systems has been running for 2 years, then nobody
knows for sure if the system will work properly immediatetly when
rebootet or some process or logical will be missing. And Murphys
law says that if there is this problem the risk of an unplanned
reboot will increase by  afactor 1000 while the system manager is
on vacation !

Arne



