In Article <35ad5745.1935408@news.pacific.net.sg>
netsurfer@sentosa.singaporemail.com (Kevin Lai) writes:
>How can I ensure all new file creation default to a fixed file
>permission?
>Currently, I had users of same Group creating files to certain
>directories, but there are some files which will be accessed by others
>and need to be modified.
>But due to the permission of the file created, the users of the same
>group still unable to modify or overwrite.
>I tried setting permission of the directories to /PROT=(G:RWED) but
>still files are created with (G:RE).

The PROCESS creating the file must have the /DEFAULT protection set so:
  SET PROTECTION=(G:RWED)/DEFAULT
(or set the protection via RMS calls from the program).

In general, a better approach to sharing files is to use resource identifier
( see HELP ADD /ident in authorize),
and set a default ACL on the DIRECTORY file, granting access for the holders of
that identifier.

