Come on, on a busy unix machine it is not exactly unusual to have
PIDs to wrap over dayly. With a 16-bit PID you only need 0.75 forks/
second to do it in 24 hours, and a medium-sized multiprocessor unix
system can easily do several hundreds. With 32-bit PIDs it is getting
a bit harder - a busy system doing 1000 forks per second will need 
about 49 days to wrap *that* over. Still, the 50 days uptimes are 
not hard to come by.

