|> Come on, on a busy unix machine it is not exactly unusual to have
|> PIDs to wrap over dayly. With a 16-bit PID you only need 0.75 forks/
|> second to do it in 24 hours, and a medium-sized multiprocessor unix
|> system can easily do several hundreds. With 32-bit PIDs it is getting
|> a bit harder - a busy system doing 1000 forks per second will need 
|> about 49 days to wrap *that* over. Still, the 50 days uptimes are 
|> not hard to come by.

Yes, I know.  I use Unix systems. I help maintain one of the free-Unix
ports.  Anyone who knows anything about the internals or usage
patterns of both Unix and VMS will realize that in VMS, processes are
much, much more heavyweight entities than in Unix.  Unix forks a new
process to execute each command from a shell; VMS uses a LCI running
in supervisor mode instead of a separate shell prodecss. Each
executable gets set up, runs in usermode in the same procses as the
"shell", and then on "process exit", the program gets rundown and the
CLI ("shell") regains control via a supervisor-mode exithook.

A typical login user consumes only _one_ process for the life of their
login session -- unless they did an explicit spawn.  Back in the days
of VMS 4.x and 5.x on small-to-medium vaxes, ordinary users might not
even _have_ spawn prilelege.

Your estimate of the time for VMS PIDs to wrap isnt even of the right
order of magnitude.



