sol gongola <sol@adldata.com> writes:

> What was intel thinking? You would think that by the time 
> they came out with 482's and pentiums, they would have found 
> a way around the limitation.

It's not the CPU's fault.  It's the fault of the machine architecture.
The PC standard has no place for smart interrupt controllers.
A smart interrupt controller can hide lots of CPU inadequacies
(ie, the controller interrupts the CPU, then the OS queries the
controller to see what device was actually interrupted).

The real problem is, the PC standard wasn't every seriously designed.
It was all based off of quick and dirty IBM designs, with a lot of
mutually agreed upon hacks.  PCI has improved a lot of this, but that
really only covers high end devices (ie, it hasn't eliminated the need
for ISA cards, and it hasn't eliminated IRQ's).  Plug-and-Play is just
a whitewash that keeps IRQ's as they are but attempts to hide them
from the user; it's only half a solution.

> What we have today is a lot cheaper. Even though we are getting a 
> lot more for the money, you still get what you pay for.

It's the "good enough" syndrome.  If computers were cars, then
the PC is a Yugo with great upholstery, a super stereo system,
and a fiberglass sports car molding.

Back when NT was new, the typical PC had serious trouble running
more demanding OS's than Windows 3.1.  It was difficult to find a
cheap PC that would run UNIX or NT well, because the extra strain on
the hardware exposed lots of problems.

-- 
