Before getting to my own diatribe, a few excerpts from others, as background (with
a bit of interspersed editorial comment) ...

[ me ]

>>  If only the religious fanatics on both sides would acknowledge that there is
>>  no single answer that can serve as the best solution to every problem, maybe
>>  we'd see less heat and more light ...

[ JF Mezei ]

>>  And if marketing gurus at Microsoft stopped pitching NT as the end-all system
>>  that fits all needs, perhaps less sheeps would install NT for mission critical
>>  systems and NT would be relegated as a file/email server and have no really
>>  bright future.

Good point.  But I don't fault the Microsoft people for producing the hype (hey,
in this field, there's hype everywhere) as much as I fault the decision-makers
who *believe* the hype (more on this below).

[ Jamie Schrumpf ]

>>  If UNIX has been given the chance to improve in the 25 years since it was first
>>  implemented, then shouldn't NT get the same chance?

[ Brian Hurt ]

>>  Yep.  I have a test install of NT scheduled for 2018.  I'll let you know
>>  how it goes.

Heh.  Good one!

[ JF Mezei ]

>>  Unix did not pretend to be able to handle business critical applications for a
>>  long while.
>>  This phenomenon is relatively recent in UNIX's life. But Microsoft has been
>>  pushing NT as a serious platform from day one without giving it the time to
>>  mature and prove it can be
>>  used for serious apps.

[ Richard Tomkins ]

>>  I have a Windows NT Server in my office, I use it to prvoide auxilliary
>>  storage for my group. I have it on a UPS. Every 3 or 4 weeks, without
>>  notice, and without intervention, it either BSOD's or halts, dead. The
>>  oinly recovery is a reboot. There is no information anywhere in any log's
>>  or anything about what happened. With OpenVMS I'd be able to process the
>>  crash dump file and the error log and the operator log and the audit log's
>>  and probably arrive at the culprit that caused the proble and fix it. With
>>  Microsoft, reboot, does it fail now, no, then it must be one of those
>>  things.
>>
>>  One of those things? What the heck way is that to run a business?

Indeed.  And this leads me to finally bring up something I've been wondering about
for quite a while now.

Back in the late 70s and early 80s, many information system managers (or whatever
they were called back then) avoided UNIX like the plague, in large part because it
was viewed as flaky, unstable, prone to crash a lot, etc.  At the time, there may
been some justification to their fears (though most of the bad reputation UNIX had
at the time was overexaggerated).  Fine.  They stuck with whatever ran on the big
mainframes, or with VMS, or with other more robust operating systems from various
other vendors.  I can understand the basis for their decision.

But now, two decades later, most versions of UNIX are pretty darn solid.  We have
a heterogeneous environment with four different major flavours of UNIX (different
minor versions of some of them), and our systems stay up for months, if not years.
A system crash that's not attributable to hardware failure is an Unusual Event.

So what I just don't understand is why these same people (well, not the identical
culprits, of course; their counterparts twenty years later) are *still* avoiding
UNIX (which has become quite reliable), and are also avoiding OpenVMS (which has
always been pretty robust), instead betting the family jewels on something which
isn't exactly yet ready for prime time.

I could certainly understand having NT on desktops, and perhaps a few NT servers
to play around with, but if I were in a position where I had to choose a system
for some mission-critical application, I think I'd take the attitude that a wise
approach would be to wait N years (N == 2? 5? 10?) until NT hits some reasonable
reliability threshold, and in the meantime stick with something I could trust.

But most jump, lemming-like, over the cliff onto the NT bandwagon.  (Hey, how's
that for a mixed metaphor? ;-)  Sigh.

