> failure, cpu failure entwork card failure, disk and tape failure and still
> kept on running. All you did was buy the right hardware and add an optinal
> Fault Tolerant Layered Product to the machine and everything on the
> machine was Fault Tolerant. All other Fault Tolerant machies are software
> API set's. You have to write the application to be Fault Tolerant with a
> special set of libraries.


Although the rest of your message makes some valid arguments about
differences between older operating environments and Windows NT, and
while I also have fond memories of working with VMS in previous jobs,
I'm sorry to have to say that your representation of the VAX-FT as an
original and/or unique concept requires correction.

Stratus Computer (my present employer) pioneered the concept of
software-transparent fault tolerance.  Stratus holds several patents
on a "pair-and-partner" scheme of redundant hardware.

Since 1982, programmers of Stratus machines have enjoyed just the
type of environment that you describe above, in which an application
program does nothing special when a CPU or other hardware component
fails.  And this works today with the VOS operating system, with FTX
SVR4 Unix, or with Hewlett Packard's HP-UX Unix. The fault-tolerant
hardware (and to a small extent, the operating system) take care of
the problem--invisible to the user's application software, which does
NOT have to roll back or fail over or any such thing.

Other vendors have adopted similar ideas, but the VAX-FT came out LONG
after the first Stratus FT200--and never sold as well as DEC had hoped.


> ...
> ...your boss read all the hype in Byte Magazine and elsewhere...

Apparently not THAT many bosses were reading BYTE; the magazine was
recently sold off by McGraw-Hill and the new owners applied the Jack
Kevorkian Theory of Management.  I attended the wake.  :-(

