In article <6oehm5$vlc@r02n01.cac.psu.edu>,
Bryan Jensen  <bjj+2@arlvax.arl.psu.edu> wrote:
>While it's clear that Intel engineers were architecturally impaired, 
>their interrupt controller chip did allow for the possibility of active
>low, level triggered interrupts, should IBM have been determined enough
>to do so.  That would have solved the problem.

The *really* foolish thing was thinking they had to use the 8259A in the
first place!  It's a leftover from the 8080A, there's no reason (from the
CPU's point of view) why you can't have 256 different IRQs, just by building
a little logic to spoon-feed all the different INT opcodes to the CPU when it
acknowledges an interrupt.  The CPU can be oblivious to 256 priority levels
just as easily as 16!  Not having the CPU participate in interrupt priority
was one of Intel's biggest blunders.  Not that PC IRQ priority is done in
any sensible way in the first place...

Not having per-ring SPs is another huge blunder.  What were they thinking.

>Perhaps IBM was copying the VAX edge triggered interrupts.  The UNIBUS
>would have been quite beyond IBM's comprehension, but they might have
>picked up on just the edge triggered thing.

Actually, IBM has built Unibi for their 43xx machines, so they really *should*
have known better.  But I think they had no idea the PC would do so well,
they just wanted to slap together a quick-and-dirty machine to mop up what
they thought would be an insignificant corner of the market, and they chose
the 8088 precisely because it works with all those nasty old brain-dead
8080A support chips that have made our lives hell.

John Wilson
D Bit



