Unix did not pretend to be able to handle business critical applications for a
long while.
This phenomenon is relatively recent in UNIX's life. But Microsoft has been
pushing NT as a serious platform from day one without giving it the time to
mature and prove it can be
used for serious apps.

UNIX's battle against the hackers is about over and it has survived and
plugged a lot of security holes. Its got battle bruises to show. NT is just
starting to enter the battlefield
and while it has some idea of what kind of battles to expect, it had no idea
on whether it 
will be able to tackle them.

Yes, Microsoft will plus the holes as they are discovered. But companies who
build critical systems now on NT risk being the guinnee pigs that discover
those holes. I call that risky business.

NT may progress quite rapidly, faster than UNIX did. It will catch up. But
during this period,
those who invest in NT may find their investment more expensive than expected
with substantial and frequent upgrades during that period.



