The Provisional Airport Authority of Hong Kong, who built Chek Lap Kok
Airport, made a strategic decision to 'standardise' on NT way back in
'92 or '93. Presumably they are still stuck with that decision in
operating the new airport - the chances are pretty good that the
embarrassing need to re-open Kai Tak for HACTL cargo traffic just days
after CLK opened was caused by NT.

Presumably the Kai Tak operations were running the old, clunky Unisys
mainframes - they just kept on running. Anyone heard of good ole'
fashioned "RASS" - Reliability, Availability, Serviceability,
Scalability?

The darn thing (Chek Lap Kok airport) opened a year late anyway. I
wonder if that was due to M$ and the NT strategy too? 

I recall they were using AutoCad on DOS to estimate the volumes of rock
available for the hard fill of the runways. Because of the 16-bit
limitations ("no-one will ever need more than 640kb" - W. Gates) they
had to make the measurements in small lumps and add them all up. This
introduced small "end errors" in every section that mounted up over the
massive project. The company I was with at the time did some
complementary calculations using "real engineering software" (MOSS) on
real machines (Suns) and gave a totally different value - it made a
difference of several metres in the height of the runways and hence a
fortune in the amount of extra rock that would need to be bought in. Or
they could have underwater runways at high tide!

Crickey - do you think the traffic control systems are running NT too???
Free ticket to Hong Kong? No thanks, think I'll go to Gueilin instead -
much safer!


