How about system tuning. OpenVMS and UNIX can be tuned by a good Systems
Manager to accomodate the workload. This is a evolving process over time,
say a few weeks at best and a few months at worst.

Windows NT, it's self-tuning. It's all over the map and although it may
hit it for a few seconds or so, the moment the workload changes in a
second or so and it's all out of tune again.

With OpenVMS, you could run File Services for PC's, Print Services and
database applications and mail, all off of one engine.

With Windows NT, the best you can get for optimization is to break out all
the above operations onto seperate engines.

So, one OpenVMS machine can do many of the baove at the same time, but for
Windows NT, you'd best get seperate servers.

In fact, under Windows NT with seperate servers for everything, you have
less crashing than if you try to run multiple functions on a machine.

I have a AlphaServer 4100, that runs File Services and is also used for
some interactive logins. Just booted, the system is fairly fast to log in
to. Over the course of a few weeks, it takes longer and longer to log
into, when it get's to be about a minute, I reboot the machine. I have two
CPU's and 2GB of memory in this system. If you look at what has happened,
Windows NT has tuned itslef for mostly idling with occasional File
Services and the Porgram Manager has been swapped out. Windows NT used
almost all of memory for caching, even though the demands on the machine
did not warrant it. Thank goodness that with OpenVMS, I have the
capability to control the granularity of memory usage, the way I see the
real world and not the way the OS may perceive the world at any single
point in time.

rtt



