It might just be a contribution of the hardware architecture 
failings. Only 15 interrupts available, many of which were 
already assigned internally, others like sound cards married to
specific IRQ's! No provision for multiple devices sharing an 
interrupt! 
If not for multiple SCSI devices, the architecture would
have run out device support capsblity and died long ago. 

WAY BACK when we had PDP-8's (practically toys but still 
capable of time sharing) and PDP-11's, We had to settle 
for a limit of 64 devices addresses. 

What was intel thinking? You would think that by the time 
they came out with 482's and pentiums, they would have found 
a way around the limitation.

Sorry for the off thread rambling but:

Yeah, bad drivers, bad hardware, and specialists that aren't
can really mess things up. We didn't have that in the old days. 

We did have computer manufacturers with proprietary buss's that
wouldn't let anyone else sell devices for their hardware. And
manufacturers that threatened to void a service contract 
if you put someone elses more compact/faster/better and cheaper 
memory into their CPU box. Thats gone.

What we have today is a lot cheaper. Even though we are getting a 
lot more for the money, you still get what you pay for.



