Boris (borisspamno@pleasemovil.com) wrote:
: 
: People complain about stability problems with NT. From my
: experience (developer's rather than sysadmin's) all NT
: stability problems can be attributed to unstable device
: drivers. 

I don't doubt that some such problems are the fault of device drivers.
They run at a very high privilege level, esp. in NT 4.0.

What can be done to solve that problem?


: Just curious: on VMS can a buggy audio driver crash OS for
: example? How about Unix?

Can't speak for VMS, as I have no experience there.

On Linux, the *nix with which I'm most familiar, a sound driver
invoked as a module can't bring down the kernel; if it segfaults, only
that process dies, not the kernel itself, and it can be easily and
safely restarted.  As for sound support compiled into the kernel, I'm
not sure.  In theory any bug in the kernel could bring it down - one
reason why modules are preferable to compiled-in support - but I've
yet to hear of stability problems attributed to sound drivers.

Now, there are some minor problems with video support in the X Window
System, particularly with respect to cards that don't release
sufficient specs to allow open-source drivers to be written.  X can
and does go down this way.  However, server-oriented processes
generally don't run under X, and thus continue running without
interruption even if X must be re-started.  The kernel does not die. 

The same might have been true of NT, if they had designed the GDI,
Kernel and User services to have been somewhat separate from one
another, and had implemented more comprehensive and robust memory
protection.


Joe



