  So on the one hand, you are saying that microsoft doesn't sell or
support the older versions of Windows NT (3.1, 3.51) and on the other
hand you are saying that it is easier to find support for Windows NT?

  If I make a mission critical system using in-house development
tools plus third party applications, I'm not going to want to
"upgrade to the latest version" just because I found a bug in the OS. 
I'm going to want a patch for that bug so I don't have to re-qualify my
entire production system.
  
  Also, once I've got something that works, I'm going to want to keep
it for 10 or 20 or more years without significant change.  Computers
are a tool to aid business, not an end in themselves -- so long as the
tool works (especially if it's a tool I rely on) I'm not going to want
to hear that I have to throw it out.

  Mainframe vendors have been very good at providing this kind of
support.  Some unix vendors have been good at providing this kind of
support (HP).  Microsoft, on the other hand, with it's 3 year
life-cycle for operating systems, has been terrible.  Unless a company
is in the business (and makes money from) redeveloping and
re-qualifying and re-testing their computing infrastructure every 5
years Microsoft tools are disastrous.  You will note, however, what
business Microsoft is in...
