[ Michael D. Ober ]

>>  I sincerely hope what you're doing is trying to build a business case to
>>  determine the best computing platform for your environment.  Although I'm an
>>  NT supporter, I don't always recommend NT (or even MS products) to people if
>>  it doesn't fit their needs.  Not all OS's are good for everything.

Indeed!  And though I've been a self-confessed UNIX zealot for nearly twenty
years, I totally agree.  A lot of the verbiage trying to pass as discussion
is reminiscent of the UNIX-vs-VMS flame wars that have popped up, off and on,
for a couple of decades.

If only the religious fanatics on both sides would acknowledge that there is
no single answer that can serve as the best solution to every problem, maybe
we'd see less heat and more light ...

>>                                                                      As for
>>  your case against NT, I would throw out any problems that aren't
>>  reproducible on NT 4.0  (i.e., the British bank example in this thread),

Also, I would suggest throwing out any problems which aren't demonstrably the
fault of NT itself.  For example, someone suggested the fiasco at the new Hong
Kong airport.  But does anyone really know whether these problems stem from a
problem (or problems) with NT, or is it simply a case of bad system design, or
poor software engineering, or plain old sloppy programming, or something else?
I certainly don't, but would be interested to find out.

Whatever, unless NT itself really *is* the culprit, it would be intellectually
dishonest to use it as an example:  Lousy programmers and system designers can
make a mess of anything, on any platform.

