Content-Type: text/plain; charset=us-ascii
Organization: Digital Equipment Corporation
Mime-Version: 1.0
Newsgroups: comp.sys.dec

Try "setld" (see man setld(8))

For example, to see what software subsets are installed, try:

  setld -i | grep installed

To see what is in a particular subset:

  setld -i <SUBSET_NAME>

where <SUBSET_NAME> is something like OSFBASE425, etc.

You can use the "inventory" command (-i) to setld as a
normal user, but you need to be the superuser in order
to use the load or delete commands.

Hope it helps.

-Jeff
