From: reece@eco.twg.com (Reece R. Pollack)
Subject: AlphaStation 200 4/233 scsi (in-)compatibilities
Date: 1998/07/08
Message-ID: <35a7bfaf.70777312@news4.his.com>#1/1
X-Deja-AN: 369628714
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 7bit
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=us-ascii
X-Authenticated-User: reece
Organization: Heller Information Services
MIME-Version: 1.0
Newsgroups: comp.sys.dec,comp.os.vms

Like several folk who inhabit these newsgroups, I purchased an
AlphaStation 200 4/233 from OnSale. My choice for a disk drive was the
Quantum VK34550S Viking, a 4.55 GB, 7200 RPM drive. I ran into an
interesting problem with using this drive that may be of interest to
others. I'm using the SRM console, so ARC console users will have to
translate.


Symptoms:

1) The drive does not show up on a SHOW DEVICE listing for a couple of
seconds after the INITIALIZE self-tests. If the system is configured
for auto-boot the initial boot attempt fails, saying that it cannot
locate the drive.

2) After a second or two the drive appears on the SCSI bus, with the
jumper-assigned SCSI unit ID. A manual boot attempt will succeed, as
will the first automatic boot retry. Once an OS is booted from the
drive, the drive's unit ID does not change. No errors are reported,
and performance is good even under the heaviest load conditions.

3) If the system is allowed to remain at the console prompt, the
drive's SCSI unit ID will wander. Successive SHOW DEVICE listings will
show the drive at various SCSI unit IDs; on occasion the drive does
not appear in the list at all. Attempts to manually boot from the
drive will succeed, if you can identify the drive's SCSI unit ID.

4) The SCSI unit ID changes appear to occur after the periodic bus
activity that these units exhibit when sitting at the console prompt
(ie after the front panel disk light flashes).

5) Both Quantum and Digital thought this was a SCSI termination issue.
An active SCSI terminator was acquired for the external bus interface
connector, and the Viking drive was moved to the end of the internal
SCSI bus cable with termination enabled. This had no effect on the
problem.


Solution:

The Quantum Viking disk drive supports a protocol called SCAM, or
"SCSI Configures AutoMagically". This is a multi-vendor protocol that
implements a sort of plug-and-play for the SCSI bus. There is
apparently an incompatibility between the NCR 810 SCSI adapter on the
Alpha's motherboard and the Viking's SCAM implementation. Disabling
SCAM on the Viking cures all the problems described above.

Quantum tech support can supply a DOS-based programs to disable SCAM
on their disk drives. Of course, this requires you to temporarily plug
your drive into an Intel-based system. Perhaps someone will write a
program to do this that runs on an Alpha.

Since SCAM is a mult-vendor protocol, this same problem may show up
with other vendor' products. If your system exhibits these symptoms,
see whether your disk drive supports SCAM.

-- 
Reece R. Pollack
Senior Software Engineer
Attachmate Specialty Products Group



