|> [brg@soda.CSUA.Berkeley.EDU (Brian Gaeke):]
|> 
|> > Your kernel may be missing the driver for the PMAG-B. Try booting
|> > /genvmunix or /usr/genvmunix as a workaround, and then reconfiguring
|> > the kernel to support an fb0 device. I ran into a problem like this
|> > on one of our DECstations.
|> 
|> What I failed to mention was that I am not serving the machine ULTRIX
|> at all.
|> 
|> When it boots up with the PMAG-EV (no discs, just the PROM), I have a
|> "proper" picture (telling me it is trying to fetch the kernel (etc) with
|> tftp), but when I put a PMAG-BA in instead, I only get a black screen.
|> 
|> Now, I would think there were different PROMs for different
|> videocards,

If you're saying the CPU board has different PROMs for different
monitors, that's wrong.  Each TC video board (every TC board) has a
PROM signature and an onboard PROM which supports self-test callbacks
from the CPU PROM.

The DECstation PROMs have a default search order in which they search
the TC slots looking for a PROM video device. If rusty memory is
correct, lower-numbered slots have priority.


|> but if I put the PMAG-BA in the thirds slot (the EA takes
|> the two first) it goes into color tests, like it should.

Sorry, do you mean the PMAG-E(V?) goes into colour tests,
or the SFB does?

|> That puzzles me. Why doesn't it then do a similar selftest when it is
|> in slot 0?

Did you have monitors connected to both framebuffers?  I though that
both would, if you have monitors on them, but I'd guess that if only
one card is doing the selftest, which card it is depends on the slot
order of the cards.

If all else fails you hook up a VT100 compatible to the serial console
port, do a "setenv console s", then powercycle, and see which
framebuffers do the self-test.  I'm pretty sure a PMAG-B will:).



