Quantum or Seagate disk drives are the fare used today by the SBU. Your
milage will vary, between an off-the-shelf unit and a DIGITAL unit.

OpenVMS interacts with it's hardware intimately and it will not be able to
do that properly, if at all, with an off-the-shelf unit.

off-the-shelf units do not impliment the full SCSI spec that DIGITAL uses,
so in some cases, system problems can occur. One example of a problem area
is Windows NT will sit on a disk and read the data 50 or 100 times and say
nothing about the difficulty in getting the data from the drive. OpenVMS
on the other after 3 tries is looking around for a replacement block, so
your valuable data doesn't just suddenly disappear one day. OpenVMS is
more robust than other OS's and thus demands more of it's peripherals. A
DIGITAL disk drive is tested and qualified both forwards and back and is
guarenteed to work. AN off-the-shelf unit is not qualified nor guarenteed
to work with either the hardware or software.

rtt



