> here is likely to have less than half the performance of Alpha on x86
code.


I believe this will turn out to be true. Alpha will still be the king in the
server world. Probably at the same time Merced is introduced, 21364 will be
there and deliver even more than 2 times performance of Merced (supposed
Merced can meet its core frequency.) However, manufacturing and production
quantity are still Intel's advantages.

> Intel apparently "borrowed" some of DEC's Alpha hardware tricks (hence the
law
> suit), maybe it should try the same shortcut with FX!32 technology to
boost
> Merced performance on x86 code  ;-)  Or maybe the real reason for the
Merced
> delay is because Intel wanted to beef up its x86 hardware.


Those 10 patents infrigement that then DEC filed were too vague, I do not
believe Intel stole any "new" microarchitecture ideas from Alpha. Most of
the ideas such as out-of-order, dynamic br. prediction, speculative, .. were
there many years ago, you can trace back to IBM 360 times. I think FX!32 is
not the right way to go for Merced's IA-32 backward compatibility simply
because we are talking about 2 ISAs concucted from the same company. (FX!32
for Alpha is different, from DEC to Intel, 2 companies, 2 different
interests.) Hardware emulation through dynamic translation from x86 to IA64
is more likely to happen in Merced in order to deliver the performance level
that F.Pollack's claimed as well as migrate people from IA32 to IA64 in a
smoother manner. However, the chanllenge would be how to translate/bundle
x86 into IA64 3-tuple template ? How to generate code dynamically with
speculative instructions and so on, so forth. There are a lot of research
needed to be done in this pretty new area. I can't wait to see how Intel
addresses this chanllenge. Illinois's IMPACT group had done something on
this, but I still like to see a commercial product with this type of
innovative technologies.

Bulldog







