I understand the VMS and VMS applications are ready for the 
year 2000 (for the most part), however what about older 
_hardware_?  For example, I run VMS on a VAXstation 3100/M76 
and VAXstation 3100/M38.

The M76 has a DS1287A clock module which is not year 2000 
compliant - it is basically an MC146818 (with a lithium
battery) that stores the year as a two-digit BCD number.
It is, however, in a socket which would seem to imply that 
the designers planned for a hardware upgrade (say, the 
DS12C887, which is pin-compatible with the DS1287A but is 
Y2K compliant).  Does VMS (V7.1) correctly handle the DS12C887
in these older workstations?  Or maybe I should first ask,
What Y2K-compliant clock module is used in the newer 
VAXstations?

The VAXstation 3100/M38 and VAXstation 2000 both use the
MC146818, which is not Y2k compliant.

