> and VAXstation 3100/M38.
> 
> The M76 has a DS1287A clock module which is not year 2000 
> compliant - it is basically an MC146818 (with a lithium
> battery) that stores the year as a two-digit BCD number.
> It is, however, in a socket which would seem to imply that 
> the designers planned for a hardware upgrade (say, the 
> DS12C887, which is pin-compatible with the DS1287A but is 
> Y2K compliant).  Does VMS (V7.1) correctly handle the DS12C887
> in these older workstations?  Or maybe I should first ask,
> What Y2K-compliant clock module is used in the newer 
> VAXstations?
> 
> The VAXstation 3100/M38 and VAXstation 2000 both use the
> MC146818, which is not Y2k compliant.

As far as I know, Digital/Compaq have stated that all Vax hardware has no
Y2K problems related to the battery backup clocks.  While my Internals and
Data Structures manual is a little old, the implication is that the external
clock need be little more than an interval timer, with the ability to store
a time up to about 15 months from some base.  VMS itself stores its current
year information on-disk and represents the time-of-year in a 32-bit register
to 1ms precision (in addition to the proper 8-byte internal time at 100ns
precision).  At an individual processor level, reading or writing this
register will cause translation to or from the external representation used
by the clock chip.  I wouldn't be surprised to find that the "time" is
transferred to and from the clock in binary format, and not BCD at all.

This rather strange design can probably be attributed to the fact that the
first Vax was designed in the late 70s, when calendar chips probably didn't
exist.  If so, then its very basic assumptions have probably resulted in
automatic Y2K compliance, in contrast to much more recent PC/BIOS
(mis-)implementations.

