|>It is, however, in a socket which would seem to imply that 
|>the designers planned for a hardware upgrade (say, the 
|>DS12C887, which is pin-compatible with the DS1287A but is 
|>Y2K compliant).
|>          Does VMS (V7.1) correctly handle the DS12C887
|>in these older workstations?  Or maybe I should first ask,
|>What Y2K-compliant clock module is used in the newer 
|>VAXstations?
|>


During the bootstrap process VMS reads the time of day register (TODR) 
and validates the result. It also keeps track of the last time a $SETIM
was done in the SYS.EXE image. AFAIK, the century contents of any RTC are not
used. So there should be no problem.

I've certainly performed Y2K testing of various MicroVAXen from the same
era without encountering any problems (although I din't actually use any of
the VS3100 series in my work).

I do not know what RTC is used in the later VAXen, but I don't think it
matters for the reasons stated above.

As always, these are just my personal opinions and YMMV.

Antonio

