In article <35A4AD5F.65AF16EF@__m-net.arbornet.org__>,
Jerry  <__jerome__@__m-net.arbornet.org__> wrote:
>I understand the VMS and VMS applications are ready for the 
>year 2000 (for the most part), however what about older 
>_hardware_?  For example, I run VMS on a VAXstation 3100/M76 
>and VAXstation 3100/M38.
>
>The M76 has a DS1287A clock module which is not year 2000 
>compliant - it is basically an MC146818 (with a lithium
>battery) that stores the year as a two-digit BCD number.

You're making the common mistake that "either the hardware is not
Y2K compliant, or the software is not Y2K compliant", when in
reality what you have to be concerned about is the combination of the
two.  In the case of VAXstation clocks, VMS has for a long time known
how to "roll over" the clock hardware.  Testing I did with VMS 5.4
showed that it not only handled the 1999->2000 transition, but
it also handled 2099->2100, 2199->2200, and I would guess it would
handle future century rollovers without a hitch, though if future
transitions are important to you, I'd advise that you do your own
testing!

The rollover logic isn't that hard to implement; it's clearly outlined
in the DS1287A/MC146818 documentation.

Tim. (shoppa@triumf.ca)



