JF Mezei wrote:

> Starting next year, there should be a permanent space station in microgravity,
> right ?
> 
> So, at what point does it become economically feasable to build chips in space ?
> 
> Transport of these light/small materials should not be such a problem. And up
> there, you need not worry about a truck passing nearby ruining a whole batch
> of CPUs, right ?
> 
> Is this still science fiction, or is this getting close enough that
> accountants should take a look at it to see if it might not be feasable ?

Experiments in this direction have already started.  For example, the
University of Houston's Space Vacuum Epitaxy Center (where I used to
work) has flown a device on the Space Shuttle called the Wake Shield,
designed to use molecular beam epitaxy to grow GaAs and YBCO thin films
in ulta-vacuum microgravity conditions. 

The International Space Station is irrelevant for such efforts (except
perhaps from a servicing standpoint), as a manned space station would
have too many vibrations from astronauts bungling around the cabin,
which would result in crystal defects...
-- 
