Monty Brandenberg wrote:
> 
> Zalman Stern wrote:
> >
> > JF Mezei (jfmezei.spamnot@videotron.ca) wrote:
> > : What is so wrong with doing like Apple did when it migrated from 68k to PPC ?
> > : Built-in automatic emulator that kicks in when an "old" piece of code is activated.
> >
> > This is a vastly harder problem in Intel's case. There are multiple
> > operating systems. They don't control any of them.
> 
> Another factor frequently overlooked is the need to support on-board
> bios code for installed options during POST, boot, and by some operating
> systems.  (Oh, you wanted *options* with that system?)  Solutions are:
> 
> .  Hardware emulation
> .  Emulation in system bios code (a path partially used by Alpha)
> .  Merced-specific cards
> .  Move card support into system bios
> .  Dump legacy bios design *cough*


(Hi Monty)

 To continue this side rathole...

 The solution that Apple took on the PPC for this specific problem
 was to use OpenBoot (IEEE 1275-1994) - which is essentially
 binary encoded Forth.  I believe its ROM type 1 in the PCI spec.

 OpenBoot came from work done at Sun, and Forth is a interpreted
 (or should I say incrementally compiled) language - and thus
 independent of the ISA it executes on - the vendor provides a
 the basic Fourth implementation in its O/S or ROM.

 It also makes it easy (or much easier) to call the ROM from the
 O/S after boot.

 Standardization on OpenBoot - and dumping the x86 BIOS, CMOS setup,
 ARC, ARCBIOS, Alpha SRM, etc. would be a significant boon to a lot
 of us low level system developer types.

 There is a thin-layer implementation of ARC for WNT that calls
 OpenBoot functions... be great is MS could use their muscle and
 make OpenBoot the standard.



