I work in As/400 development lab for IBM although my response should be
considered an unofficial response (that makes our attorneys happy). Wanted to
expand on/clarify some of the info in the previous response. Intent is
information/help not sales. (sorry for long response) More information on
AS/400, at http://www.as400.ibm.com. 1.  AS/400 runs OS/400 operating system
which includes integrated DB2/400 database, mail server, internet support,
transaction processing, communications, security, job scheduler, disk
management, work management, backup/recovery, etc... This is a different
operating system from IBM AIX.	AIX runs on an RS/6000 and is a unix
operating system.  Though OS/400 supports most unix apis, it is not branded
"unix". 2.  NT runs on an integrated Intel ancillary processor on the AS/400.
 The AS/400 is a certified platform for NT.  Would have to understand better
what you want to do on NT to understand if separate pc server is needed. 
Whether you need NT at all (inside or outside of AS/400) depends upon if
there are applications available on NT that are not available on AS/400. 3. 
RPG is one of several languages on AS/400.  As/400 supports RPG because of
protecting customer investment in application development and because a set
of customers still use it.  AS/400 customers also use Powerbuilder, Delphi,
Visual Basic, etc.... w/odbc and/or ole-db connection.	This is possible
because DB2/400 is a standards compliant SQL database.	Also available for
appl. dev. are C, C++, Java, basic,pascal, pl/i, cobol. 4.  AS/400 uses a
64-bit processor with 64-bit operating system, 64-bit database, 64-bit
middleware and 64-bit applications.  As mentioned in previous commentary
64-bit processors are "very fast".  Due to unique As/400 technology,
customers had auto-migration of most pre-64bit applications to 64-bit without
rewrite and/or recompile of appls.  Therefore AS/400 has over 25,000 64-bit
applications.  (Applications need to be 64bit to take advantage of 64bit
hardware;  otherwise it's like a 64 lane highway with half the lanes closed.)
