I have the feeling I'm doing someones homework.  If the question
had been "I have an XXXXX GB file in YYYYYY application.  Am I
going to have trouble with any of the following operating
systems?", it would not require a major research project and book
length dissertation to answer.  Please simplify your question and
repost.  You also left out Linux EXT2, CIFS (SMB), NFS, and DFS
complications.

I'll bite on the SCO part of the puzzle.

SCO is a company, not a product.  SCO has 4 major Unix OS
products that support about 10 different filesystems on three
different storage technologies.  Add various RAID technologies
and the number of combinations are mind-boggleing.

Storage technologies:  IDE, EIDE with LBA, SCSI, ATAPI.
Products:  Xenix, OpenServer 3 (3.2v4.x), OpenServer 5
(3.2v5.0.x), Unixware 2, Unixware 7.
Filesystems:   S51k, AFS, EAFS, HTFS, DTFS, VxFS, RockRidge, ISO
9660/High Sierra, Xenix, various DOS mutations, NFS (yes, it's a
filesystem), NUCFS (Netware Unix Client FS), various ramfs
implimentations, etc.

For numbers and explanations, see:
What are the filesystem types that can be used in SCO OSR5?
   http://www.sco.com/cgi-bin/ssl_reference?105852

Disk drive capacity and filesystem limitations under SCO UNIX 3.2
Version 4.x
   http://www.sco.com/cgi-bin/ssl_reference?104467

FS limits for Xenix are bit difficult to find.  Xenix IDE does
not support LBA and is therefore limited to 2GB maximum
partition, filesystem and file size for both IDE and SCSI.

If you enter "filesystem" into the site search at:
	http://www.sco.com
you will get 140 hits on the topic including:
	http://www.sco.com/products/baseos/family/filesytc.htm
which is a multi-page white paper explaining everything you ever
wanted to know about SCO OSR5 supported filesystems including a
comparison chart.

Unixware 2.x support yet a different conglomeration of filesystem
types.  See:
   http://www.sco.com/unixware21/2.1.2/sys_overview.htm
for general overview.

