"M. Cao" <mcao@u.washington.edu> writes...
}There are someone try to break in my OpenVMS system.
}I check the OPERATOR.LOG file, but this log does not
}record any login failed attempts status.
} 
}Would you tell me how to set the OPERATOR.LOG, which
}include all login status.
} 
}Minh

First see if you have alarms/audits  enabled for breakins with a SHOW AUDIT
This should show a line that looks something like this:
(VAX VMS V5.5-2:)
Security alarms currently enabled for:
[...]
    BREAKIN:    (DIALUP,LOCAL,REMOTE,NETWORK,DETACHED)

(or Alpha VMS V6.1:)
System security alarms currently enabled for:
[...]
  Breakin:       dialup,local,remote,network,detached
[...]
System security audits currently enabled for:
[...]
  Breakin:       dialup,local,remote,network,detached

If you are running V6.1 (or possibly 6.0) or higher and have the security
alarm enabled then you'd get reports in OPERATOR.LOG that look something like
%%%%%%%%%%%  OPCOM  10-JUL-1998 05:52:58.0 %%%%%%%%%%%    (from node X at
 10-JUL-1998 05:52:58.00)
Message from user AUDIT$SERVER on X
Security alarm (SECURITY) and security audit (SECURITY) on X, system id: 1.3
Auditable event:          Local interactive breakin detection
Event time:               10-JUL-1998 05:52:58.0
PID:                      20A000BE        
Process name:             DECW$LOGINOUT   
Username:                 <login>         
Process owner:            [SYSTEM]
Terminal name:            _WSA1:
Image name:               X$DKA300:[SYS10.SYSCOMMON.][SYSEXE]LOGINOUT.EXE

(a remote breakin should include info on where it came from other than just
the terminal name like this local event has).

You can also search the file for the text "breaking" and "failure" (there
are "interactive login failure" events as well as "interactive breakin
detection" events).

If alarms are not enabled but audits are then these may not happen (under
pre V6 versions, this is aparently not an option).

Note that if you are checking a satellite node in a cluster, then all the
operator log entries may not appear there. I have my cluster set so that
most of the ndoes don't keep their own operator log, but their events
appear in the log of a boot node. If youin a cluster you may have something
simillar - if this is the case then there will be nothing in your particular
node's operator.log file since when this was set up.

Then you can analyze the security audit journal. Try
ANA/AUDIT/EVENT=BREAKIN SYS$MANGER:
(the SYS$MANAGER: points to the directory the audit data is stored in, the
file name will be filled in with the default info; you can skip this if you
are in the SYS$MANAGER directory by default, such as you tend to be when logged
in as SYSTEM).

Add a /FULL to that to get the full data instead of the brief list, should
there be any. You might also want to use a /SINCE and/or /BEFORE to narrow
down the selection criteria.

There could also be some useful info in the accounting log, check for
accounting events logeed at the same time as the audit events (done
via ACC/SINCE=date-time/BEFORE=date-time/FULL in a window arouind the
event time).

--- Carl



