Jay Maynard wrote:
> 
> On Tue, 07 Jul 1998 01:36:13 GMT, Ben Myers <benmyers@ultranet.com> wrote:
> >I'd have to agree with you as ZLXp-L relates to Linux.  Linux needs
> >drivers written for it so that whatever graphics card will work.  The
> >ZLXp-L was made for DEC by Dynamic Pictures, who may well be willing
> >to release specs for their graphic chip.
> 
> Ah. Has anyone asked?

Just some clarifications...  The original TurboChannel versions of these
cards and the chips were designed at Digital by a group in Palo Alto.
After that project, DPI was spun out/off of Dec.  When a PCI version was
needed, DPI did much of that work.  Who owns the IP at this point in 
time I haven't a clue.

> 
> >Could it be that Linux "supports" the Cirrus
> >Logic SVGA chip, and simply ignores the real 3D graphics engine on a
> >ZLXp, either E or L?
> 
> I can't speak for the L, since I don't have one; the E is supported not as a
> VGA, even in text mode, but as a TGA, both in MILO (the Linux/Alpha
> MIniLOader) and in XF86. Were I to guess, however, I'd guess you could waste
> the L's true capabilities and treat it as a Cirrus Logic, at least until a
> chip-specific X server comes along. You can get a CL board for one hell of a
> lot less than a ZLXP-L, though, I would guess...

The original TGAs were developed for both TC and PCI and do not have anything
like a VGA interface in them.  System bios needs to support console operations.
The PCI successor, TGA2 or 3d30 and 4d10, present a VGA device and can be 
used generically.  They'd be ideal cards for a 2D system as they can easily
produce 30+ Xmarks if you know how they work.



