JF Mezei wrote in message <359D1959.5D0BF6D2@videotron.ca>...
>does anyone know for sure if merced/ia64 will have an embedded 8086
hardware
>emulator (as well as PArisc) or will Intel use an FX!32 type of software
>solution ?

There were some patent applications that Intel filed not so long ago,
patenting some of the technology in Merced. It basically outlined Merced
instruction set, and how Intel intends to do the mechanisms for X86
instructions as well as PA-RISC, and native Merced all on one core. So
unless Intel decides not to follow the mechanisms outlined in these patent
applications, then it's a safe bet that it'll all be in hardware.

>If Intel really intends to embed an 8086 and PArisc in the IA64, it will
>undoubtedly make the IA64 a very versatile chip, capable of running 3
>architectures. However, the mind boggles as to
>the amount of complexity required to build 3 chips in one. Sounds to me
like
>this is
>going to be a very big chip that's going to cost a lot of silicone and

>generate a lot of heat.

Intel's had a lot of practice at it already. The P6 architecture is
basically an entire RISC chip core built for the specific purpose of X86
emulation. (Of course, the AMD K6 and Cyrix 6x86 cores are also the same
idea).


>Now, if they really do embed an 8086 in the chip, obviously, work is
already
>in progress, and by the time IA64 comes out, the 8086 in there will
probably
>be old compared to the
>8086s that are going to be on the market at that time.

It's not likely going to be an entire X86 core just to do X86 processing.
It'll just be an instruction decoder stage that breaks down the X86
instructions into smaller more basic native instructions, just like what is
happening with the P6 processors now.

>What is so wrong with doing like Apple did when it migrated from 68k to PPC
?
>Built-in automatic emulator that kicks in when an "old" piece of code is
activated.

Because it was slow, and people were annoyed by it.

>What is wrong with doing like APPLE does for some of the PC emulation on
MACs:
>put in a
>small daughter card with an 8086 chip in it ? This way, Intel wouldn't have
to
>jam so much into that big IA64 and would be able to supply an up-to-date
8086
>chip on a daughtercard when it ships IA64 machines.

Because Intel is selling manufacturers a single CPU, it's not selling a CPU
and an APU (ie. Auxilliary Processing Unit).

