In article <6nte5b$6qc$1@nnrp1.dejanews.com>, mikemarsh@my-dejanews.com writes:
|>Spoke with a DEC field rep yesterday and was told the info I'm requesting is
|>proprietary!
|>

He's probably right. However, you are probably asking the wrong questions :-)

There is no "bus" internally in a VAX 4000-108 into which you can add 3rd party
cards, although there are slots available for a few options like the DSW43.
If you have a technical guide for one of the older VAX 4000-100/105/106 machines
then I recall that the pin-outs for the internal expansion connectors are
described there. The bus details are not described (and are probably of no
interest) and the pin-outs will have changed since the 108 uses different
connectors, but at least you'd know what signals are there!

In terms of externally accessible busses, you have DSSI and an optional 
Q-bus. Usually there is SCSI via an HSD10.

I believe that the 108 is simply a 106A respun so that it fits into the
standard PC-tower cases. So performance probably matches the 106A: approx
215 tps (10ns cycle time, 512KB Bcache). The only difference I can see is
that the 108 can have up to 512MB of memory installed.

