But this is a religious holiday, not a government-declared holiday.  The
date is determined by a formula adopted by the Catholic Church many 
centuries ago, and as far as I know, accepted by all Protestant churches.
(But not by Eastern Orthodox churches, who generally celebrate Easter a
week or two later.)

I'm not sure about the "full moon" bit, though.  The vernal equinox and 
the full moon are both astronomical events and independent of any 
calendar.  ISTR that this calculation is actually based on a "theoretical 
full moon" and a "theoretical vernal equinox", both of which have drifted 
from the actual astronomical events over the centuries.

Certainly, if the the western Easter falls on the 1st Sunday after a full
moon, the eastern Easter, two weeks later, can't!

This year, according to my calendar and The Old Farmer's Almanac, Easter
fell on April 12, and Orthodox Easter on April 19.

According to Guy Ottewell's Astronomical Calendar 1998, the formula for 
Easter is the 1st Sunday after the 1st full moon after the vernal 
equinox, but the vernal equinox is assumed to always be on March 21
(not always astronomically correct), and the assumed date of the full 
moon is also not always correct.

Of course, it is also time-zone dependent.  If the full moon occurs on
Sunday, then Easter would fall on the next Sunday, but when it is full
moon and Sunday here (in USA Eastern time zone), it might still be 
Saturday on the West coast, or it might already by Monday in Jerusalem or
Rome or where ever the calculation was based.

John Santos



