In article <6nqsse$t5t$1@nnrp1.dejanews.com>, atadashi@my-dejanews.com writes:
> The HSJ50 Storage Works Device controllers have the ability to store the     
> current time in the conventional format, dd-mmm-yyyy hh:mm:ss.  Why?

        The HSJ50 (and other HSJ's?) store  a log of errors encountered.
    You  can  review  this  log of errors (and clear  them,  etc.).   In
    addition, the controller keeps a log of when it was  last  shutdown,
    booted,  etc.   For  all of these, the date-time is an "interesting"
    piece of information that most users  of that information would like
    to  have.   However,  AFAIK,  the date-time is in no  way  used  for
    controller operations.

>                                                                       I have 
> been pouring through the manuals trying to find out if it is possible to set 
> the contollers time to match the system time at start up.  Why?

        The controllers are independent  systems.   They  have no direct
    knowledge  of  "the system time", as you call it, on any  given  VMS
    host.

>                                                                  At this     
> point in time, I cannot see any link between the controllers and the OS with 
> regard to "sharing" any time references. 

        They don't.  Well, at  least  mostly  they don't.  Somebody else
    will  need  to  confirm/correct this, but I believe that  there  are
    date-time messages of some  sort  present  on  the  CI,  perhaps  by
    request  only.  If my (weak) memory serves, a controller booted into
    a CI with already active VMS hosts will retrieve the host's time (or
    _some_ host's time).  Typically,  however,  one  boots all the HSC's
    and  HSJ's _before_ booting VMS hosts (e.g., after a power outage or
    similar).  In this case, at least for HSC's, the  date-time  reverts
    to   some   default  (I  don't  think  it's  the  VMS  base-date  of
    17-Nov-1858, but I also don't remember the last time I checked...)

>                                           In fact, when I first started      
> looking into the CLI commands for the controllers, I was surprised to find a 
> particular set of controllers showing the current date as, dd-Oct-1995.  It  
> didn't seem to affect the file operations taking place on the system.        

        This "time discrepancy" has been present  for a long time (since
    the  first  HSC's?), so it shouldn't be surprising that  it  doesn't
    affect file operations "taking place on the system" (and you  really
    need to specify _which_ system you're talking about here), otherwise
    the  problem  would  have  been identified and corrected a long time
    ago.

> This is a Y2k issue, since, according to Yourdan & Yourdan, any device that
> can have the date set is a potential Y2k problem.

        The key word here is "potential".  I think this case (CI storage
    controllers) falls in the same category as the digital clock on your
    microwave oven: it may  display  only  two  digits (not the case for
    HSJ's),  or  the  time  may  be wrong, but  it  doesn't  affect  the
    microwave's operation in any  way.   Of  course,  to  be  absolutely
    certain, you'd need to talk to a CI storage engineer...

> Does anyone know if OpenVMS AXP (v7.1) or OpenVMS VAX (v6.2) is capable of   
> referencing/setting the time on the HSJx0 controllers from the system startup
> files?  I cannot use VCS (Polycenter software) since someone decided to let  
> the license lapse (or we just never purchased it to begin with).  The DECK   
> DCL command doesn't work for the SET HOST/DUP and even if it did, I don't    
> suspect the CLI on the controller would understand, F$Cvtime(,"absolute").   

        Make sure that FYDRIVER has been loaded, e.g., in SYCONFIG.COM:

    Alpha:

	$ sysmanio = "$SYSMAN IO
	$ SYSMANIO CONNECT FYA0/NOADAPTER -
                               /DRIVER=SYS$LOADABLE_IMAGES:SYS$FYDRIVER.EXE
    VAX:

        $ sysgen= "$SYSGEN"
        $ SYSGEN CONNECT FYA0/NOADAPTER

    Then use the following command to connect to the HSJ50 named "HSJ001":

    	$ SET HOST/DUP/SERVER=MSCP$DUP/TASK=CLI HSJ001

    This certainly works for me. :-)  

> Finally, does anyone know why this time element exist when it seems to have
> little if no impact on the mission of the controller: data transfer and
> storage??

        See above.  I thinks it's a convenience and error analysis aid.

            -Ken
