Jay,

I'd have to agree with you as ZLXp-L relates to Linux.  Linux needs
drivers written for it so that whatever graphics card will work.  The
ZLXp-L was made for DEC by Dynamic Pictures, who may well be willing
to release specs for their graphic chip.

Another interesting wrinkle to consider re. "support":  I examined a
ZLX-L2 a little closer today and found a very vanilla Cirrus Logic
SVGA chip on the board, so the board can at least boot up in
Intel-centric text mode.  Could it be that Linux "supports" the Cirrus
Logic SVGA chip, and simply ignores the real 3D graphics engine on a
ZLXp, either E or L?  That would be a serious waste of at least 2 1/2
dimensions of the 3D, wouldn't it? ... Ben 

jmaynard@thebrain.conmicro.com (Jay Maynard) wrote:

