Oh well...perhaps I put a bit too much faith in that sweet-talking
UPS gal.  On June 24th she phoned as I was writing a demand letter
(a love note that states that if the receiving party doesn't
make good on some obligation, there's gonna be a law suit); she
tells me that UPS at it's expense will reship my computer back to
Bozeman.  That was 11 days ago.  It only takes 8 to make the
crossing :-)

Shooting clay pigeons is a bit of an art...you have to gauge just
how far to lead your target before you pull the trigger.  I'm
looking for info just slightly before I _might_ require it.

Suppose you were in a room, and there was a uV3400 providing your
computing requirements.  Suppose further, that when you were out of
the room on your regularly scheduled coffee-break, a meteor crashes
through the ceiling and destroys your uV3400 and all the installed
software (the tapes were sitting atop the unit).  Just the box is
demo'd.  The monitor and any peripherals are OK.  The boss walks in.
looks at the damage, pulls out a check-book, signs a check, hands it
to you and says:  "Replace it, and give me the receipt."

So what would be the replacement cost of the 3400 (2-800 MB disks, a
TK-50 tape drive, and all of the TK-50 tapes that constitute the
installed software)?  Now the limitation here is, that it has to be
another VAX running VMS so there are software and licensing costs. 
I've tried to find Great Lakes via the WWW and haven't succeeded. 
The replacement could be a rebuilt 3400, license, and software (at
what cost), or any other formerly DEC now Compaq piece of hardware
etc. that will provide equivalent service (let's say that the demand
on the box was single user access to the net, to computational and
plotting software, as well as email -- not some mission-critical
function for business or government).  Keep it reasonable...this may
very well wind up in a courtroom.


  Mark  


