The HSJ50 Storage Works Device controllers have the ability to store the
current time in the conventional format, dd-mmm-yyyy hh:mm:ss.	Why?  I have
been pouring through the manuals trying to find out if it is possible to set
the contollers time to match the system time at start up.  Why?  At this
point in time, I cannot see any link between the controllers and the OS with
regard to "sharing" any time references.  In fact, when I first started
looking into the CLI commands for the controllers, I was surprised to find a
particular set of controllers showing the current date as, dd-Oct-1995.  It
didn't seem to affect the file operations taking place on the system.

This is a Y2k issue, since, according to Yourdan & Yourdan, any device that
can have the date set is a potential Y2k problem.

Does anyone know if OpenVMS AXP (v7.1) or OpenVMS VAX (v6.2) is capable of
referencing/setting the time on the HSJx0 controllers from the system startup
files?	I cannot use VCS (Polycenter software) since someone decided to let
the license lapse (or we just never purchased it to begin with).  The DECK
DCL command doesn't work for the SET HOST/DUP and even if it did, I don't
suspect the CLI on the controller would understand, F$Cvtime(,"absolute").

Finally, does anyone know why this time element exist when it seems to have
little if no impact on the mission of the controller: data transfer and
storage??

