Eric Christy <christy@pa.uky.edu> writes:

>As I stated, my machine was already upgraded to the 4/233 before Ipurchased  it.  I'm
>not quite sure how the ISA and PCI bus speeds
>are set, since DEC has never put such information in their official
>documentation (or anywhere else that I can find).  I would certainly
>be happy if someone who knows (possibly someone from DEC who
>has worked with this machine?) would share this information.

I worked on this system when it was being developed.  To change the
processor speeds you need to change the CPU, the oscillator and a PAL
chip. The PAL is the gotcha - it controls the access to the outside world
and is specific to the speed.  To operate at 275 MHz you'd have to develop
(and debug) a custom PAL for that speed.

I don't know how much heat a 275 MHz chip would give off vs. a 233 MHz
one.  However I do know that except for the parts mentioned and some cost
savings items (lower cost graphics card, no floppy or CDROM by default)
the AS 100 is the same beast as the AS 233.

>Actually, I just wanted to clock the cpu from 233MHz to 275 MHz, notdouble it.  As I am
>sure the experts from DEC know, the cpu in this box
>runs at half of the oscillator speed.  I am therefore looking for a 550 MHz
>crystal.

The oscillator is a standard part.  But again the gotcha is the PAL...

-Mike



