Dan Pop wrote:
> 
> In <359CE3AF.22AE@world.std.com> "Terry C. Shannon" <shannon@world.std.com> writes:
> 
> >Of course, the dates upon which some holidays are observed are
> >determined by government decree: plenty of date-adjustment to ensure
> >three-day weekends.
> >
> >Happy Fourth of July (another US-only holiday)
>        ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^
> Is this one subject to date-adjustment, too? :-)
> 

No, the Fourth is the Fourth, just like the Fourteenth is always
Bastille Day in France. The date-adjustment refers to the weekday upon
which a work holiday is granted in observance of the event. Used to be,
if we had a mid-week holiday (such as the birthday of one of our dead
presidents), the day off occurred on the calendar date of the holiday.

Some years ago, "THEY" made a decision to **observe** the holiday on a
Friday or Monday. But the Fourth of July always falls on the Fourth.





