JF Mezei (jfmezei.spamnot@videotron.ca) wrote:
: What is so wrong with doing like Apple did when it migrated from 68k to PPC ?
: Built-in automatic emulator that kicks in when an "old" piece of code is activated.

This is a vastly harder problem in Intel's case. There are multiple
operating systems. They don't control any of them. Both the performance and
compatibility requirements are much more stringent. (E.g. Merced likely has
to run an x86 OS "binary" untouched. Apple's strategy depended on key parts
of MacOS running native to deliver performance.) Any solution must be
easily exportable to hundreds of vendors, some of whom have minimal
software engineering resources. (E.g.  good luck getting a significant
number of drivers rewritten before FCS.)

Intel's one asset is that they get to define the hardware. (Much more so
than Apple or anyone else could change PowerPC at the point the Power
Macintosh project started.)

So far as I can tell, Apple still hasn't seen fit to roll Software FPU (or
equivalent) into the system software. (For the non-Mac contingent, the 68k
emulation sfotware that is part of the Power Macintosh system does not
emulate the 68881/68882 floating-point hardware. There is a third party
utillity from John Neil Associates that does this called Software FPU.) I
assure you that Merced would be soundly trounced if it only ran x86
programs that didn't have FPU instructions. To me, this characterizes a
significant market difference between the two situations...

The DEC and HP transitions of the last decade or two are also worth looking
at.

-Z-



