From an article by Jennifer B. Malapitan in the Metropolitan Computer
Times (http://www.newsbytes.com/news/98/114352.html, but you need a
password) Joel Berman, director for Unix and high performance
computing for Digital Unix in Asia Pacific, says that Digital Unix
will be adopted by Tandem as its IA64 Unix.  He also goes on to say
that Digital Unix will be available on IA-64 from ``day one,''
whenever that happens to be.

I guess this is really old news, as Compaq has said such before.
Though I was pleased to see in the article that Berman says the alpha
will stick around as long as it has a performance lead.  In addition,
the article states that ``Digital never designed Alpha to be the
dominant platform since its launch in 1993,'' so hopefully Compaq has
realistic expactations about alpha's place in the market, and won't be
dissapointed when it doesn't out sell the Pentium II by Labor Day,
whenever that happens to be.

Though I do not use VMS, I was very dissapointed to see that it will
be disappearing on workstation class machines.  I use workstation
