In article <359B8F3A.1D7517FC@pa.uky.edu>, Eric Christy <christy@pa.uky.edu> writes:
..
:  I have an alphastation200 4/100 with a 275 MHz chip in it running at 233.
:It was upgraded when I bought it but they evidently ran out of the 233 chip
:and used the 275 instead.  I would like to find an 550 MHz or 600 MHz
:oscillator but have been unsuccessful thus far.  If someone knows, please
:let me know.  Also, is this all I need to do?  Can I overclock by just an
:oscillator replacement?

  If you wish to try to fry your system, sure...

  Please visit the archives and look at the previous discussions around
  overclocking systems.  There have been various other discussions of the
  pitfalls of overclocking over the years...

  You will want to be aware that just because one chip of a system may be
  rated to run at 275 MHz, the rest of the system -- memory, I/O busses,
  etc. -- may not operate as expected at that clock rate...  And you cannot
  assume that the 100 MHz system design has the cooling that may well be
  necessary for overclocking.  (By way of example, an alcohol-powered racing
  engine "chip" won't work as expected in a Model T "system" -- the system
  designs just aren't compatible...)

  And you should not expect that doubling the clock rate will even operate
  on a given system -- oh, if running a system design or a chip that much
  faster were that easy...

  Also see the previous discussions around what the folks over at Kryotech
  went through to cool an overclocked (767 MHz) Alpha microprocessor...

