Steve Loughran wrote:
> [snip]
> I'm curious which exhibits the greater speedup when moved from x86 classic
> to both Alpha and Merced:
> 1. A win32 app, cross compiled (FX!32) or executed in Merced's x86 mode.

 1) Alpha 21264 + FX!32

   For the sake of argument consider MDR's estimated future performance
   of Alpha from the latest Microprocessor Report (which tends to have
   an anti-Alpha and pro-Intel slant so might even be conservative):

   1999:  0.25 um  50 SPECint95 and 75 SPECfp95      Native code
   2000:  0.18 um  65 SPECint95 and 100 SPECfp95 

   The target for FX!32 is 70% of native performance for integer code and
   50% for floating point code (the existing version is close to this by
   most accounts).  Using these assumptions on the above estimates gives:

   1999:  0.25 um  35 SPECInt95 and 37 SPECfp95      x86 via FX!32
   2000:  0.18 um  45 SPECInt95 and 50 SPECfp95

 2) Merced

   The original claim for Merced x86 performance was it would be at least as
   fast as the fastest x86 native CPU shipping at the same time.  However, at
   last year's Microprocessor Forum, Intel rep Fred Pollack back pedalled and
   claimed that Merced would have the x86 performance of the *mainstream* x86
   devices shipping when Merced ships.  Since at this time Merced was on track
   for 1999 shipping this might translate to a 500 MHz Katmai CPU for "main-
   stream" x86 which would place it around 20 SPECint95 and 20 SPECfp95 as a
   rough estimate.

 So unless Intel is deliberately understating Merced x86 performance, it appears
 that Merced would have had barely more than half the Alpha's performance on x86
 binaries *if* it had shipped in 1999.  But since it has slipped to 2000 Merced
 will likely come against 21264's manufactured in the same 0.18 um process and
 here is likely to have less than half the performance of Alpha on x86 code.

 Intel apparently "borrowed" some of DEC's Alpha hardware tricks (hence the law
 suit), maybe it should try the same shortcut with FX!32 technology to boost
 Merced performance on x86 code  ;-)  Or maybe the real reason for the Merced
 delay is because Intel wanted to beef up its x86 hardware.

> 2. A java application moved from x86/NT to Alpha/NT and Merced/NT
> Does anyone have any real data?

 The Merced Java compiler/VM is probably receiving a lot more attention than
 can be mustered for the Alpha version and this might counter balance the
 faster Alpha device so it is anyones guess.

> 
>     -Steve

All opinions strictly my own.

