does anyone know for sure if merced/ia64 will have an embedded 8086 hardware
emulator (as well as PArisc) or will Intel use an FX!32 type of software
solution ?

If Intel really intends to embed an 8086 and PArisc in the IA64, it will
undoubtedly make the IA64 a very versatile chip, capable of running 3
architectures. However, the mind boggles as to
the amount of complexity required to build 3 chips in one. Sounds to me like
this is 
going to be a very big chip that's going to cost a lot of silicone and
generate a lot of heat.

Now, if they really do embed an 8086 in the chip, obviously, work is already
in progress, and by the time IA64 comes out, the 8086 in there will probably
be old compared to the
8086s that are going to be on the market at that time.

What is so wrong with doing like Apple did when it migrated from 68k to PPC ?
Built-in automatic emulator that kicks in when an "old" piece of code is activated.

What is wrong with doing like APPLE does for some of the PC emulation on MACs:
put in a 
small daughter card with an 8086 chip in it ? This way, Intel wouldn't have to
jam so much into that big IA64 and would be able to supply an up-to-date 8086
chip on a daughtercard when it ships IA64 machines.



