: does anyone know for sure if merced/ia64 will have an embedded 8086 hardware

yes, someone does know for sure.  all such people work for Intel or HP,
and they're not talking.

: If Intel really intends to embed an 8086 and PArisc in the IA64, it will

that would be stupid.  the P6/PII proves that it's eminently doable
to interpret ia32 on a fairly generic, superscalar RISC core.  since
EPIC is a simply a VLIWish such core, it's a damn good bet that Intel/HP
will implement the x86 compatibility that way.  the PA-RISC is relatively
simple, and can probably be done easier.  this is not to say that Intel/HP
will ignore the benefits of binary recompilation, even doing it JIT.
binary translation is complementary to emulation, and Intel/HP may well
provide hardware support to _transparently_ execute partially translated
code.  (that is, translate the hot spots, emulate the cold ones.)

: What is so wrong with doing like Apple did when it migrated from 68k to PPC
?
: Built-in automatic emulator that kicks in when an "old" piece of code is
activ
ated.

you don't seem to remember how many problems this caused,
or how slow it was for the first _years_ of the PPC's existence.
note that this kind of emulation wasn't news when Apple did it.



