I can only speak for myself, but I refuse to relegate DECUS to "no
further use" status.

> 
> What is most needed now is a group very much focused on VMS, not a general
> "Compaq products" users group.  Most VMS shops don't give a fig what
> happens to DU, and even if they do, the group that lobbies for that should
> be separate from this anyway.  Moreover, it is perfectly ridiculous to have
> a Compaq specific group for the discussion of WNT issues, or even Linux on
> Alpha issues.

While the idea of separate groups may have merit, the ability of small,
independent groups (vs DECUS SIGs, for example) to influence Compaq is
very questionable.

> 
> I'm not suggesting that the new group look anything like the old DECUS.
> Rather, I kind of like the idea of an organization which serves as a
> discussion and planning area for the three primary VMS interest groups:
> 
>   1. Large site users (MCI, Credit Lyonnais, etc.)
>   2. VMS market vendors (hardware, software) (honorary, nonvoting seat to
>        Compaq as the primary vendor).
>   3. Small site users (academia, home users, small businesses)
> 
> Most of the bandwidth in this newsgroup has been coming from members of the
> second and third class (which, is often how we feel we're being treated!).
> Realistically, it is only an organized delegation from the first class which
> could get Compaq to sit up and take notice.  (Or, on a remote chance, a
> guerilla action by the others, which is what this billboard/picketing talk
> amounts to.)  

Guerilla action will certainly get Compaq's attention. But it is equally
certain that such action will not precipitate positive vibes in Houston.

> It isn't just that "suits only talk to suits", but these big
> boys are the only ones with any real financial leverage.  If I threaten to
> make my area "Compaq free", nobody will care, but if the first class folks
> lay down the law, they can exert some serious financial pressure.  Imagine
> an announcement where N>>1 Fortune 500 companies announce as a group that,
> due to vendor intransigence, they have decided to boycott all further
> purchases of Compaq products.

Not very likely to happen. More likely than a boycott of Microsoft since
there are plenty of alternatives to Compaq, but still not very likely.

> 
> Now you might argue that large site users won't care what happens to small
> sites, but I think that most of them are not that near sighted.  They need
> only look around their VMS groups and see where these folks came from to
> get an idea of the importance of training on smaller systems, and to
> consider where trained support might or might not come from in the future.
> And of course, even sites with the largest machines often need smaller
> machines to develop and test on, and the needs on those smaller machines
> are pretty much the same as the needs of any smaller user. Also, the large
> sites probably appreciate the fact that every hardware and software bug we
