Digital's mistake was a very simple one driven by the company's aversion to
layoff's.  The KO day it decided to go "up market" to generate the margins
needed to cover Digital's overhead costs is the day VMS's fate was sealed.
If he had gone down market and did whatever it took to deliver the price
performance ratios of UNIX and PC's he would have had all the time in the
world to prepare for assaulting the corporate data center.  Instead by going
up market he created a price bubble at the low end which folks like SUN
happily took advantage of.  Digital was now being chewed at from the bottom
while IBM kept the pressure on from the top.  You can either be the premium
player in the market (IBM) or the commodity player (MS today) but get
yourself caught in the middle and you die.  Unless you are damm sure you
will end up the premium player always go down market...






