From: Paul DeMone <PaulDeMone@EasyInternet.net>
Subject: Re: Alpha or Merced: which will run x86 code faster? (was Re: Integer sizes for NT/64
Date: 1998/07/03
Message-ID: <359D8642.2974@EasyInternet.net>#1/1
X-Deja-AN: 368556932
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 7bit
References: <3583BF17.61F0687@knobisoft.de> <3583cd68.35246011@news.primenet.com> <cogvvqks7.fsf@ite127.inf.tu-dresden.de> <Ev2Gnq.1Lz@hplb.hpl.hp.com> <sjcEv4GJx.2xq@netcom.com> <EvH02s.4oF@hplb.hpl.hp.com> <359C63E9.60B9@EasyInternet.net> <359D1959.5D0BF6D2@videotron.ca>
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=us-ascii
Organization: UUNET Canada News Transport
Mime-Version: 1.0
Newsgroups: comp.arch,comp.sys.dec,comp.sys.intel


JF Mezei wrote:
> 
> does anyone know for sure if merced/ia64 will have an embedded 8086 hardware
> emulator (as well as PArisc) or will Intel use an FX!32 type of software
> solution ?

  Intel has said quite clearly that x86 support would be in hardware.  From
  what little is known about Merced is that it actually resets to x86 mode
  and also enters x86 mode on exceptions.  This will allow it to run existing
  x86 OSs without change (although not a particularly cost effective method
  of doing so.

> 
> If Intel really intends to embed an 8086 and PArisc in the IA64, it will
> undoubtedly make the IA64 a very versatile chip, capable of running 3
> architectures. However, the mind boggles as to
> the amount of complexity required to build 3 chips in one. Sounds to me like

  Maybe Intel is boggling too and that is why Merced keeps slipping.  BTW,
  there is no public committment to running PA code in Merced hardware.  It
  is likely that some type of PA code reformatting or binary compiler system
  will be used to run PA object under IA-64.  A FX!32 type system for PA to
  IA-64 wouldn't be nearly as difficult as for the arcane and condition code
  intensive x86 ISA.  The IA-64 ISA will likely be very similar to existing
  RISC designs, and PA most of all.  In fact DEC offered this capability
  years ago for porting VAX and MIPS binaries to Alpha.

> this is
> going to be a very big chip that's going to cost a lot of silicone and
> generate a lot of heat.

  Silicone hmmm?  *Thats* why Merced keeps slipping, Intel can't get a grip
  on things (possibly a protocol bug in their weak mammary ordering model) or
  even stay abreast of Alpha.  BTW, I can barely resisting throwing in a Xena
  (Xeon) joke or two.

> 
> Now, if they really do embed an 8086 in the chip, obviously, work is already
> in progress, and by the time IA64 comes out, the 8086 in there will probably
> be old compared to the
> 8086s that are going to be on the market at that time.
> 
> What is so wrong with doing like Apple did when it migrated from 68k to PPC ?
> Built-in automatic emulator that kicks in when an "old" piece of code is activated.
> 
> What is wrong with doing like APPLE does for some of the PC emulation on MACs:
> put in a
> small daughter card with an 8086 chip in it ? This way, Intel wouldn't have to
> jam so much into that big IA64 and would be able to supply an up-to-date 8086
> chip on a daughtercard when it ships IA64 machines.

  The intriguing thing about the split personality chip like Merced with
  intimate data sharing capabilities between x86/IA-32 and IA-64 modes is
  that maybe x86 code isn't meant to go away.  After all, IA-64 is supposed
  to have about 33% larger object code than existing RISC ISAs which in turn
  have roughly 30% larger code than x86.  Maybe x86 will be a compact method
  of encoding infrequently used or non critical code segments of IA-64 apps
  in the same way that THUMB and MIPS16 offer for ARM and MIPS respectively.

  Heck, just think how big a native IA-64 "Office 2001" would be with 70% code
  expansion added in to normal M$ feature bloat ;-)

All opinions strictly my own.

--
Paul W. DeMone                 The 801 experiment SPARCed an ARMs race
Kanata, Ontario                to put more PRECISION and POWER into
demone@mosaid.com              architectures with MIPSed results but
PaulDeMone@EasyInternet.net    ALPHA's well that ends well.




