> The Titan itself never became a product, but we learned a lot from
> the experience, and much of what we learned did make it into systems
> such as Digital UNIX and the Alpha processors.  Remember that the
> Titan was a research project, and very few research projects make
> good products (nor should one expect them to).
> 
> -Jeff

A couple of other comments on Titan: it was done at DEC WRL (Western
Research Labs), not at DEC SRC (Systems Research Center) (previous mail
was incorrect on this).

Among the things it taught were:

	1) The C programming language has made it almost mandantory that
	addresses be "byte" addresses. 
	2) you don't, for most applications, need byte instructions.
	Alpha added them later, when it became clear that translated or
	emulated code from x86 systems would find them very useful; if you
	have source for a program, you can do just about as well living
	without byte instructions (and maybe keep a key critical path
	of the system faster, allowing for a higher clock rate).  

As I remember, the first 4 Titans built were word addressed, and porting 
UNIX and X clients to them was a major pain; the later systems were byte 
addressed, and things then ported easily.  I remember the hassles of getting 
the X library to run properly on the first Titans; life was too short.
				- Jim



