In article <359cce69.1162263997@news.jps.net>, SpamDelendaEst@my-dejanews.com (Bruce Lane) writes:
:	Fair warning. You will need either an active net connection OR an AUI
:loopback connector attached to that plug if you expect the system to boot.
:I found that one out the hard way! ;-)

  I've successfully bootstrapped various OpenVMS VAX and OpenVMS Alpha
  systems with no transcievers and no network connections.  Works fine.

  DECnet will log ping-pong messages if it has been started on a "bad"
  Ethernet port, but it is easy to bootstrap the system conversationally,
  and shut down the DECnet startup completely, or simply disable the 
  Ethernet line using NCP.  Something like the following NCP commands 
  will disable all DECnet access to a DEQNA or DELQA controller...

    NCP> SET LINE QNA-0 STATE OFF
    NCP> DEFINE LINE QNA-0 STATE OFF
    NCP> SET CIRCUIT QNA-0 STATE OFF
    NCP> DEFINE CIRCUIT QNA-0 STATE OFF

  The terminator is required if you don't want the irritation and the
  overhead of the ping-pong messages.

	--

  If the original poster wishes to use IP to communicate with those other
  systems, one of the various OpenVMS IP packages will be required...

