Timothy Cullip wrote:
> 
> If a person purchases a Digital Personnal Workstation (PWS) 600a
> (the "a" indicating it is an MS NT machine), can he in the future
> convert it to Digital Unix (in effect making it a 600au) by just
> a software change?

<snip>
> 
> What is involved in changing from the ARC console to the SRM console
> software (and vice-versa)? How/where would one get the SRM software
> (does it only come with the purchase of Digital Unix?)?
>
You need to flash the firmware to get the SRM console.  The firmware
does come with the DU media (yours for the bargin price of $400).
I think someone told me you could download it off the DEC web site,
but I don't know for sure.
 
> Is there any hardware difference between the 600a and the 600au?
> Both would be using the same graphics board (the PowerStorm 4D51T
> is supported by both OS's). They both seem to (from the descriptions
> I've seen) use the same SCSI controller, same Ethernet controller, etc.
> 
> Is there anything about the Alpha processor itself that is different
> for NT verses Unix.
> 

I haven't seen any.

> Has anyone actually attempted/succeeded/failed in doing this?
> 
I have switched from the ARC to SRM console.  It was a piece of cake.
Didn't do it with your particular machine, though.

> The main reason I ask is that the "au" version is basically $1800
> more than the "a" version, so if the Unix (and licenses) are
> available through our campus for < $200, then it doesn't make
> sense to purchase an "au".

Are you sure about this ?  At all the universities I've been at, you
need to purchase the 'OSF-BASE' license yourself, to the tune of
around $750.  Under the CSLG program you can get the DU OS CDs,
which you can install, but the system won't be useful until you
get the OSF-BASE license keys from a separate source.  If you have
a cheap way to get *that* I'd sure like to know about it.  

I'd check into that carefully.  I've had software licensing offices
tell me 'oh yeah, you can get the OS from us' only to find out that
you needed to the the OSF-BASE license from somewhere else.

Jeff



