I'm trying to coax back into life a DECstation 5000/125 under OpenBSD 2.3.
I've run 2.2 on my trust PD5000/25 for a while, but I'd like to upgrade to
something with a color framebuffer.

I know the PMAGB-B isn't technically supported but it's pretty close to
the other DEC framebuffers of that era so I have some hope that it can
be made to work.  The interesting thing about this particular card is that
it was jumper selectable between two modes - 1280x1024x8 and 1024x768x8.
This one in particular claims to be a -BA to cnfg with the standard 130Mhz
crystal and a 69Mhz alternate.  I think this would really make it a -BC
but I'm not sure.

The OpenBSD kernel detects this as a 1280x1024 framebuffer:
  # /sbin/dmesg | grep fb
  sfb0 at tc0 slot 1 offset 0x0 (1280x1024x8) (console)tc_enable mainbus0 sc c0402700 slot 0x1

In 1280x1024 mode the text console works fine.  However, Xcfbpmax apparently
sees this as a 1024x768 framebuffer --- the bottom third of the screen isn't
ever touched and its obviously writing stuff into video memory with the
wrong horizontal resolution.  The actual byte encoding stuff seems fine -
I was able to do things like 'xsetroot -solid red' and it appeared the
correct color.

In the 1024x768 mode things are a bit stranger.  The console is completely
scrambled (again it's obviously writing into video memory with the wrong
horizontal resolution - consecutive scan lines show up in a staggered
effect).  Xcfbpmax works fine EXCEPT that the mouse cursor appears ~150
pixels to the right of its actual position.

I'd like to get this beast working in 1280x1024 w/ X.  Is there any way
to trick X into trying to use a different type of framebuffer than the
one it thinks you have (I suspect a PMAG-BA would be a close enough fit
to work)?  Any hints at all?

Time to start downloading the source...

-Mitch



