In article <ZHKWFq5zey14@mwk>, gleason@mwk.com (Lee K. Gleason) writes:
> (I'll leave it to a unix fan to do an unflattering description of what
> a VMS house would be like...)

No such luck...

In a VMS house, you'd only need one screwdriver: a #2 Phillips. Everything
in the house would use the same screws: the refrigerator, the stereo, the
car out in the garage, even the backup utility.

All the books on the bookshelves would be the same height and color (unless
you've still got version 4 :-) ), instead of a random mismatch.

You wouldn't have to put on special underware in order to wear your favorite
shirt to work. Nor would there be a requirement to wear one pink sock on odd
tuesdays.

In the kitchen, the silverware would all be soreed in those little dividers,
and not dumped randomly in a drawer somewhere. The glassware would all match
and be nested in a cabinet over the sink, while the plates were all neatly
stacked in the next cabinet. And all of it would be made of a robust nearly
shatterproof material.

And when you left your house and went across the street to the neighbors
house, you'd find everything worked the same over there. You wouldn't need a
different screwdriver, bookshelf, underware, or glass to live in that house.

Bob Kaplow



